WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.500
It's amazing to see this room filled like
this. It's super exciting. I'm going to emcee

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.400
the event, or moderate the event.
So the first thing I was asked to do is actually

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.090
tell you about a series of events that are
a part of the McDermott Award. You're now

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:27.240
at the event called Future of the Library.
The next one will be the keynote lecture by

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:34.630
David Adjaye on March 29th. Then, we're going
to be discussing Future of the Campus, March 30th.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:44.649
And finally, a panel on Future of the
Museum on April 28th. So that's sort of the

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:50.660
big picture of what's going to happen.
But today, it's a great pleasure for me to

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:56.539
moderate conversation on the future of the
library, its architectural possibilities,

00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:02.379
cultural, organizational, and technological
pressures as an institution, and various forms

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:09.520
of magic it presents. Magic was a word that
came up many times last night in the conversation

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:16.380
that I got to eavesdrop on. The MIT Library
Task Force, we had some of the panelists tonight.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:22.939
So I'll try not to sound like there's an inside
joke about this. But magic was, in that event,

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:29.420
related to the material and spatial dimension
of the library, to its architecture, to the

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.450
encounter with material instantiation of knowledge
and books and other information objects--

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:41.890
I learned that term. And magic seemed apt
as at least one of the terms by which to describe

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:47.220
the socializing dimension of the library,
being in it together.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:53.930
So for now, I will introduce the participants
in this event by a fairly standard bio. And

00:01:53.930 --> 00:02:01.340
I hope you won't mind that. So that we can
save space, time, for these pretty talkative

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:10.819
enthusiasts of the library, of library architecture,
and I'm relieved to say, of the book.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:18.900
First-- and it may not entirely follow the
order that we've been given for the presentations.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:27.260
But I'll introduce David Adjaye, who set up
his first office in 1994, and a few years

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:33.810
later, in 2000, founded Adjaye Associates
and immediately won several prestigious commissions.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.950
In Oslo, he designed a Nobel Peace Center.
In London, his design for the Whitechapel

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:45.080
Idea Stores pioneered a new approach to the
provision of information services. Later projects

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.280
in London included the Stephen Lawrence Centre,
with teaching and community spaces, Rivington

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.290
Place, an exhibition venue and resource center,
and the Bernie Grant Arts Centre for performing

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.959
arts.
The office, Adjaye Associates, now has offices

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:08.000
in London, New York, and Accra, and is working
throughout the world. In the United States,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.580
Adjaye is the designer of a new home for the
Museum of Contemporary Art in Denver, two

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:19.440
public libraries in Washington, the Sugar
Hill low-income housing development in Harlem,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.209
and a redesigned Ethelbert Cooper Gallery
of African and African-American art at Harvard

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:28.879
University's Hutchin's Center.
Adjaye Associates' largest completed project

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:34.580
to date is the Moscow School of Management's
Skolkovo. While the firm's current projects

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:39.379
include the Smithsonian National Museum of
African-American History and Culture on the

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:45.940
National Mall in Washington, the Alara concept
store in Lagos, the Aishti Foundation Arts

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:51.470
and Retail Center in Beirut, a new headquarters
building for the International Finance Corporation

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:57.689
in Dakar, the new home for the Studio Museum
in Harlem, the Center for Art and Culture

00:03:57.689 --> 00:04:03.110
at Colgate University, and a condominium development
for Four Seasons in Washington. And we know

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:11.209
he's on at least a couple of really important
projects right now in a competition with a

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.689
few people.
Adjaye garnered many honors and awards for

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:20.019
his work, including a prestigious Royal Institute
of British Architects Bronze Medal for Architectural

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:25.990
Students and the Design Miami Designer of
the Year Award in 2011. In June 2007, he was

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:32.620
appointed OBE, Officer of the Most Excellent
Order of the British Empire for Services to

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:39.280
Architecture. Adjaye recently collaborated
with Okwui Enwezor on the design of the 56th

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.550
Venice Art Bienniale, which is last year's
Art Bienniale. And his own comprehensive retrospective

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:52.400
exhibition was held at Haus der Kunst in Munich
and then travelled to Art Institute of Chicago,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:04.639
where it just closed in January.
Jeffrey Schnapp is Professor of Romance Languages

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:09.100
and Literature and Comparative Literature
and on the teaching faculty in the Department

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:19.190
of Architecture at Harvard's GSD. He is a
founder and faculty director of metaLAB at

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:25.270
Harvard and faculty co-director of the Berkman
Center for Internet and Society. A cultural

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.550
historian with research interests extending
from Roman antiquity to the present, his most

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:35.550
recent books are The Library Beyond the Book,
co-written with Matthew Battles. There may

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:42.100
be others, but I augmented something here.
The Electric Information Age book, a collaboration

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:48.750
with the designer Adam Michaels, Italiamerica
II, co-edited with Emanuela Scarpellini,

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.990
and Digital_Humanities, co-written with a
team of colleagues.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:57.840
His pioneering work in the domains of digital
humanities and digitally augmented approaches

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.740
to cultural programming includes curatorial
collaborations with the Triennale di Milano,

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:10.479
the Cantor Center for the Visual Arts, the
Wolfsonian-FIU, and the Canadian Center for

00:06:10.479 --> 00:06:15.669
Architecture. His Trento Tunnels project was
featured in the Italian pavilion of the 2010

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:26.389
Venice Biennale and at the MAXXI in Rome.
Nader Tehrani is the Dean of the Irwin C.

00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:32.090
Chanin School of Architecture at The Cooper
Union. He is also a Principal of NADAAA, a

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:36.759
practice dedicated to the advancement of design
innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration,

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:42.620
and an intensive dialogue with the construction
industry. Tehrani has taught at the MIT School

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.009
of Architecture-- so welcome back-- where
he served as the Head of the Department from

00:06:48.009 --> 00:06:53.289
2010 to 2014. He has also taught at Harvard
Graduate School of Design, Rhode Island School

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:59.759
of Design, the Georgia Institute of Technology,
as the Thomas Ventulett Distinguished Chair

00:06:59.759 --> 00:07:04.759
in Architectural Design, and at University
of Toronto, where he held the Frank Gehry

00:07:04.759 --> 00:07:09.069
International Visiting Chair.
As the principal and founder of

00:07:09.069 --> 00:07:14.550
Office dA-- so the first incarnation of the
office-- Tehrani's work has been recognized

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:19.580
with notable awards, including the Cooper
Hewitt National Design Award, the United States

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:23.860
Artists Fellowship in Architecture and Design,
and the American Academy of Arts and Letters

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:29.259
Award in Architecture. He has also received
the Harleston Parker Award for the Northeastern

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:34.080
University Interfaith Spiritual Center and
a Hobson award for the Georgia Insitute of

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:40.300
Technology Human Research Building. Throughout
his career, Tehrani has received 16 Progressive

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:45.840
Architecture Awards, as well as numerous AIA,
Boston Society of Architects, ID awards. And

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:53.110
in 2013, 2014, and 2015, NADAAA was ranked
number one in design for Architect Magazine's

00:07:53.110 --> 00:08:02.550
Top 50 firms in the United States. And though
it wasn't listed in this bio, which was deemed

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:08.759
provided by the speaker, probably, at some
point, to CAST, NADAA received numerous

00:08:08.759 --> 00:08:13.449
awards in the last few years for its Melbourne
School of Design, completed in 2014.

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:19.979
Right. Are you with me?
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:19.979 --> 00:08:26.729
Ginnie Cooper, recently retired from the District
of Columbia Public Library, where she served

00:08:26.729 --> 00:08:31.400
as Chief Librarian for eight years, a period
during which she was directly responsible

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:37.269
for renovating or rebuilding 14 branch libraries.
Among these are both two libraries designed by

00:08:37.269 --> 00:08:45.200
David Adjaye, as well as renovation of Mies
designed Central Martin Luther King, Jr. Memorial

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:51.640
Library. Cooper was awarded the 2013 Jefferson
Award for Public Architecture from the American

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:59.300
Institute of Architects. A librarian since
1970, Cooper worked in libraries in 50-- five

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.990
states. Five states, sorry.
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:09.870
Five states and in New York City as Executive
Director of the Brooklyn Public School-- Public

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:15.910
Library. Can you see, I'm just reading these--
She's a past president of the Public Library

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:19.660
Association, a division of the American Library
Association, and a recipient of the Charlie

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:25.120
Robinson Award, made by the Public Library
Association to recognize a public library

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.959
director who has been a risk-taker, an innovator,
and an agent for change.

00:09:30.959 --> 00:09:39.680
Chris Bourg, finally, is Director of the MIT
libraries. She arrived to MIT in February

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.570
2015. Prior to MIT, she served in a variety
of leadership positions at Stanford University,

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:49.360
where she managed the public service facets
of physical and digital library services of

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:53.940
the largest division of the Stanford University
Libraries, with six branches and a collection

00:09:53.940 --> 00:10:00.350
of more than 4 million volumes. Bourg's career
began with 10 years of service as an officer

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:04.060
in the United States Army, including three
years on the faculty of the United States

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:10.170
Military Academy at West Point, where she
taught sociology and leadership. She has written

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:16.329
and spoken extensively on the topics of libraries,
leadership, diversity, and social justice.

00:10:16.329 --> 00:10:20.420
She's a strong advocate for gender equality,
and was involved in creating and leading the

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:24.779
first Women's Voices and Influence group for
Stanford staff.

00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:35.860
All right. So, with that, we have a kind of
order. I hope that didn't put you all to sleep.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:42.550
And it begins with David Adjaye. So I think
the way we're going to run this is that every

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:49.010
one of you comes here and presents the ten-minute
presentations or responses. And then we're

00:10:49.010 --> 00:11:01.329
going to sit down and dive into, you know,
magic.

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:06.420
[LAUGHTER]
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:18.730
OK. Thank you. It's nice to be here, to be
part of this incredible award and to be talking

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:27.610
to you about libraries, which are a very important
space to have a debate about, in terms of

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:34.880
our civic and public life. I have 10 minutes.
I have too many slides, so I'm going to try

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:38.829
to keep it in 10 minutes.
Essentially, I wanted to show you the images.

00:11:38.829 --> 00:11:44.180
I'm not going to talk extensively about everything,
but to really talk about the investigation,

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:51.149
since 2000 to this time, that we've been doing
into the nature of the library. And to summarize

00:11:51.149 --> 00:11:57.510
that, essentially what has become a key driver,
is this notion of place-making. The library

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:05.100
moves from just being a depository of public
knowledge as the Internet dissolves that archive,

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:10.380
and becomes a place of socializing to gain
knowledge and network place, and a place to

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:16.870
re-imagine different parts of the city and
how it contributes to our public and civic

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.360
life.
I'm going to show four examples in different

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:31.550
parts of the world, and hopefully that will
stimulate a discussion. First one is in Gwangju.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:39.790
And probably most unusual, Gwangju has 
incredibly high levels of literacy, an extraordinary place

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:47.529
where there's a Bienniale. And we were invited
to create a pavilion in that Bienniale.

00:12:47.529 --> 00:12:55.440
I worked with a writer, Taiye Selasi, and
we imagined the idea of a library as a public

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:03.410
piece of infrastructure, the library as a
public staircase that leads from the street

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:11.279
to the river. This is a kind of quick context
image. But essentially, we conceived this

00:13:11.279 --> 00:13:16.920
idea that in this incredible culture that
really has an incredible dynamic of literature

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:23.970
and reading, that we would dissolve the notion
of building into just a place that could activate

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:30.360
engagement. It would be, you know, Wi-Fi-enabled
to deal with all the Internet issues. But also, it

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:35.380
would be a place that somehow would be able
to archive and store information, and be a

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:41.459
place to trade and share information. And
then, at the same time, also just be a structure

00:13:41.459 --> 00:13:44.639
that would go up and down to the water and
beyond.

00:13:44.639 --> 00:13:53.250
So a big experiment. It's been incredibly
successful, and we were surprised. The building

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:59.769
is designed so that it basically floods when
the high floods come, and it's in two parts.

00:13:59.769 --> 00:14:04.149
It's sort of at this river edge, and we'll
go through it.

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:12.949
So it's a concrete and timber structure. The
base is sort of the infrastructure of stairs,

00:14:12.949 --> 00:14:19.470
which also have bookshelves indented into
it with cast concrete and sealed. And the

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:27.250
upper level is this timber porous sun shade,
which creates this sort of monument. This

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:53.100
is the timber structure. And then this is
the forum.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:15:00.790
Second was the idea of a library in a local
community in London. And the idea was-- in

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:08.060
a very diverse part of London, which historically
has always had huge diversity, about 15 different

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:16.260
languages spoken formally in this neighborhood.
We were to make a new kind of a library infrastructure

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:22.500
system that would essentially try to bring
information to this community and serve as

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:31.910
a support network for the diversity of different
groups and age groups that would use this

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:35.440
infrastructure.
We sought to basically seek to make a library

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:42.190
like a textile and a network of systems that
we connect together, sometimes literal and

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:49.639
sometimes metaphorical. And to create a building
which essentially was a podium, sort of an

00:15:49.639 --> 00:15:56.480
extrusion of the street, with a series of
services in it. It would combine life-long

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:04.459
learning, it has a creche, it has dance
spaces, spaces for learning about body and

00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:12.860
wellness, outdoor spaces, cafes, and all the
various sort of parts of a traditional library

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:19.660
that you know-- media, computer studies, language
labs, et cetera.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:24.529
So it became, really, a building which has
this central core in which all the activity

00:16:24.529 --> 00:16:28.699
happens. It has the kind of double stair,
which takes you up and down. And you have

00:16:28.699 --> 00:16:34.579
a series of plates that are basically set
up to deliver these services and to dissolve

00:16:34.579 --> 00:16:45.540
the notion of the library into a perimeter
system of services that are in this main space,

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:52.480
but then a series of window moments, which
allow the user to basically turn away from

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:56.529
the building and, as it were, engage with
their environment. So there is no reading

00:16:56.529 --> 00:17:01.860
room. The reading positions are all on the
perimeter system. And then the sort of stacks

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:05.209
and systems, or teaching spaces, are inside
the forum.

00:17:05.209 --> 00:17:09.439
It was also the first time that we built a
what would be a kind of Platinum-LEED building

00:17:09.439 --> 00:17:17.770
for a library, where there were no ducts.
It works with pressurized floors and a mass

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:26.160
concrete moment structure, which was,
at that time, a big change. So to be highly

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:31.460
environmentally conscious and to also kind
of re-think the services and infrastructure

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:37.760
of this type.
It had this overhanging structure that overhung

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:42.960
the street. It had escalators that bring you
directly off the street. Multi-porous system,

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:48.870
no single entry. And the escalator would bring
you up to the quieter spaces. The ground floor

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:56.610
was more children's library, media, et cetera.
The canopy was basically devised to trap heat

00:17:56.610 --> 00:18:00.500
gain-- those of you who know about architectural
heat gain-- and to kind of create an exhaustive

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:06.460
climate moderator for the facade, which will
also work as a new kind of porch to position

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.970
the building in the public realm. We were
allowed by the city to make this building

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:17.610
jut out into away from the building line.
Because of this statement about a new public porch.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:21.650
A new piece of infrastructure, a new, modern
way of creating a public porch, which would

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:28.200
then bring you up into this building and deliver
the various services to you. It very much

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:35.870
welds with the street, which is a market street.
And it's a seven-day market, so it's always

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:39.299
there.
And then the south-facing chamber becomes

00:18:39.299 --> 00:18:45.230
this moment where, with this sort of colored
glass, you get these effects. This is that

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:49.460
chamber, the kind of moderator that people
go up and down. And it releases heat at the

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:54.200
top, and allows the building to stay cool in its interior. This is a kind of image of the

00:18:54.200 --> 00:19:03.450
interior with the exposed slabs and the way
in which the spaces unfold. Dance spaces.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:08.039
So the spaces with the vignettes.
We dissolved the notion of the book stacks

00:19:08.039 --> 00:19:12.750
and turned them into what we called a landscape,
which allowed people to kind of surf in different

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:20.910
ways. Connections between floors. And then
the rooftop, a cafe with retractable skylights

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:33.760
that creates an outdoor courtyard on the roof and gave
the citizen a new vantage point to see their

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.960
community.
And then these are two libraries that I'm

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:44.250
going to speak about, just quickly show you
and end with, in Washington that I worked

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:52.650
with Ginnie Cooper on. And they are two libraries
in two communities in southeast and southwest

00:19:52.650 --> 00:20:01.830
of DC. in Anacostia and a close neighbor. And
in a way, they were communities, one not so

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:07.700
depressed, but another that was a low-income
community. And it was about trying to imagine

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:16.210
this infrastructure that would create a sense
of social pride in the socializing of knowledge sharing

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:21.870
and create a building that would deliver
new services to these communities, which would

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:27.669
help to empower the generations in the community
in their aspirations and in their life, in

00:20:27.669 --> 00:20:30.679
a sense.
So it's really, how does a building that is

00:20:30.679 --> 00:20:34.590
a new piece of infrastructure, that's not
just a kind of utilitarian box, but something

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:42.070
which sets up something very specific, engage
with these very diverse, suburban-like communities?

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:49.870
And to create a kind of connection to this
bigger idea of what the library can offer.

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:57.409
So this is just an image of the team and some
of the libraries that were all done, where

00:20:57.409 --> 00:21:00.400
two of us are in there. These are just giving
you the images of the communities. This is

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:05.640
Francis Gregory, which is next to a park.
Some of the reference images, this idea of

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:09.929
a kind of new public space.
This building was set up almost as the idea

00:21:09.929 --> 00:21:21.010
of the public room that connects to the landscape.
Its upper levels had sort of meeting and community

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:25.900
rooms. The children's library was brought
upstairs.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:31.110
And so you have this volume within this volume.
The skin of the building is modulated so that

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:36.080
it expands and contracts, depending on your
orientation. The elevation looks like diamonds

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.470
that are all the same size, but they were
all contracting and expanding to do this notion

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:46.549
of this growth form. And the building is actually
40% insulated. It's a highly sustainable building.

00:21:46.549 --> 00:21:54.230
But it presents the perception of what glass
is as a kind of dichotomy. As you sort of

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.330
turn around the corner, you see it in a different
light. And as you come to it, you see it in

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:03.299
a different way. So it has a porous sort of
surface around it to do with storm water management,

00:22:03.299 --> 00:22:09.100
et cetera. And as you kind of move around
it, what you think is thin becomes thick.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:13.429
And as you move into it, you see that you're
in this wooded pavilion of these sort of dilating

00:22:13.429 --> 00:22:19.950
diamonds that are bathed in light.
And creates this room that becomes a series

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:27.210
of lenses to the forest and to the community.
And it becomes a room where teaching, learning,

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:32.210
and activities are happening. And the stacks
are also in the space. They're in kind of

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:41.250
enclosed chambers of different forms using
thick glass and thin glass, et cetera. Moments

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:47.990
where the form releases, they become very
vertical, vertigious, it sort of dissolves.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:56.730
And then children's library and community
spaces. The building in its context. Disappearing

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:01.980
in the winter and the summer.
And then the second, in a different community,

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:08.020
was Bellevue, which has a kind of topography
that's fascinating, because it's a sort of

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:16.600
series of hills with a very typical house-builder
architecture. Detached homes, but very fascinating

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:23.799
sort of devices of these stilts that negotiate these landscapes. And I became very

00:23:23.799 --> 00:23:27.809
fascinated by that.
And so this is the institution here. It's

00:23:27.809 --> 00:23:32.919
round about these houses. We sought to make
a form which had a kind of an agglomerating

00:23:32.919 --> 00:23:37.530
character. A series of smaller forms, and
then a larger body. Smaller forms would start

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:43.909
to create these very special rooms which would
frame the city, the community, in different

00:23:43.909 --> 00:23:51.909
ways, giving you vantage points to it, but
also would create elevations to explore. And

00:23:51.909 --> 00:23:59.669
to have a place that would be a way for the
community to re-think their own environment,

00:23:59.669 --> 00:24:04.159
but also see it as a kind of center where
they can come together. On the ground plane,

00:24:04.159 --> 00:24:10.179
volumes were elevated to create a plaza where events could happen. Students

00:24:10.179 --> 00:24:14.789
would meet under-- this is south-facing--
so it would be under these volumes, creating

00:24:14.789 --> 00:24:20.460
a sort of informal teaching, arrival, gathering
space, community space for the community.

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:25.470
The library you would come into would offer
digital and popular services on the ground

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:32.179
floor, and then you would rise up the building.
So here into different spaces

00:24:32.179 --> 00:24:40.529
and stacks. Children's library on this floor.
And that's going to rise again up to a space

00:24:40.529 --> 00:24:45.940
where you would have other stacks and sort
of arrival on the final floor, where

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:52.730
you'll be able to enter these chambers. So
this children's library has a small sleeping

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:59.210
parlor for children, which looks over its
children. Sort of a sleeping, storytelling

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:04.309
pod that would be specific to this building
or section.

00:25:04.309 --> 00:25:32.350
So it's really a kind of spiral to this view.
Here's the building in its context. The arrival.

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:46.870
Great, thanks.
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:46.870 --> 00:26:01.400
Great.
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:08.120
What I'm going to contribute to this discussion,
I hope, will nicely dovetail with the presentation

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:15.390
David has made here. And I'm giving you what
is basically a kind of 10-minute recipe version

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:22.360
of what could be an hour-long discussion about
what is the mix of elements, programmatic,

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:28.980
mission-based, the fine-grained and macro
considerations that, in my view, ought to

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:35.490
be points of reference in a conversation about
the future of libraries. And like every recipe,

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:42.120
this is one where an expert chef knows the
importance of ingredients, but also the freedom

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:47.140
that exists to combine and re-combine in ways
that make sense. And that making sense process

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:53.190
for me is all about something that I think
is exquisitely present in David Adjaye's work,

00:26:53.190 --> 00:27:00.440
which is an intense sensitivity to what it
is that place and space do in a given place.

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:05.820
The way in which they can serve as catalysts.
The way in which they resonate with the history,

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:12.950
with the context, with the entire environment
within which a library performs its function.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:17.909
So what I'm going to do here in this kind
of short 10-minute version is just walk through

00:27:17.909 --> 00:27:23.820
a couple of elements that seem to be interesting
pieces of the larger puzzle of answering the

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:29.679
question of what a library could be or should
be in the 21st century. So I'm going to start

00:27:29.679 --> 00:27:34.919
with some things that I don't think it should
be or could be plausibly in the 21st century.

00:27:34.919 --> 00:27:39.399
If you ask people at the turn of the 19th
century, entering the 19th century, and then

00:27:39.399 --> 00:27:44.610
at the beginning of the 20th century, what
a library is-- and indeed, this is an image

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:48.440
that continues to haunt our imagination when
we think of answering the question of what

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:54.770
is a library-- the answer was an Industrial-era
storage system for books.

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:59.409
And much of the history of late 19th century
libraries and many of the legacy libraries

00:27:59.409 --> 00:28:05.110
of today allocate an enormous percentage of
their interior space to this storage function.

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:10.500
Indeed, that storage function was viewed as
a kind of sublime advance in the history of

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:18.919
the library as an institution. People stood
outside of New York Public as the steel stacks

00:28:18.919 --> 00:28:22.760
were being built, and looked at them with
the same admiration that they looked at the

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:28.110
building of skyscrapers in the same era. And
I do not think we are in that era anymore.

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:32.919
So one of the exciting questions we must confront
as a community of people who are thinking

00:28:32.919 --> 00:28:38.850
creatively and actively about the nature of
knowledge spaces is, what are all the great,

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:47.190
cool things we can do with a lot of that space
that was dedicated before to mere storage?

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:52.529
The second ingredient that I don't think we
need to think about is what I think of as

00:28:52.529 --> 00:28:59.779
the consummate dumb space of the contemporary
world, which are computer workspaces. They

00:28:59.779 --> 00:29:06.970
look little more like-- they barely differentiate
themselves from tobacco-- you know, places

00:29:06.970 --> 00:29:12.890
where people were rolling cigars. I think
we can do more creative, interesting things

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:18.450
with a world where the database has become
one of the defining features of the world

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:23.470
of knowledge sharing, exchange, production,
and so forth. And one of the things, of course,

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:29.419
that the database does, and its ubiquity does--
the fact that it can be accessed through devices

00:29:29.419 --> 00:29:34.890
that may fit in my pocket, or that ride in
my briefcase-- is to raise, I think, really

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:39.250
powerful and rich and important questions
about where the library begins and ends. And

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:43.890
what it is that a place in space can do that
the database cannot.

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:49.370
Databases are not places. And the knowledge
that they convey means something or is of

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:54.260
trivial importance in relation to the places
where that knowledge is activated. And I think

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:58.940
the question about what a library could or
should be today is a question about how databases

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:02.960
intersect with local places and realities
and stories.

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:09.880
So with these two excluded ingredients, I
want to turn to a set of possible ingredients

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:16.039
that we could think of. And I already anticipated
my number one point, which is thinking creatively

00:30:16.039 --> 00:30:21.590
about storage. And that can mean a number
of different things. Of course, it isn't an

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:26.179
invitation to abandon the storage function
of libraries. I think that storage function

00:30:26.179 --> 00:30:31.590
remains vital.
But we can be selective. We can adopt a qualitative

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:37.610
approach to the idea of, what are the assets
whose physicality is a tremendous core feature

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:42.039
of the experience of a knowledge space like
the library? And what kind of things do we

00:30:42.039 --> 00:30:46.990
want those collections to do in relation to
the communities that animate and activate

00:30:46.990 --> 00:30:51.720
a space? And of course, the answer is going
to be very different in a research library

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:56.750
and in a local branch public library, because
of the nature of the kind of activation processes

00:30:56.750 --> 00:31:02.630
we're talking about.
Number two-- documents, of course, are migrating

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:07.360
into digital environments and formats, but
we are printing and producing more print artifacts

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:14.340
than ever in the history of printing today.
And so we have this whole world of hybrid

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:19.029
media, which has, by the way, always been
the world of the library, but in a new form,

00:31:19.029 --> 00:31:25.039
in a new configuration. So how do we create
spaces that allow us to work across those

00:31:25.039 --> 00:31:32.440
kinds of platforms in the way that people
actually read, think, exchange, share today?

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:39.269
That's a core question. Within that question
is a question of where we add value to, through

00:31:39.269 --> 00:31:42.980
the design of the physical spaces that are
under discussion.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:47.159
The second question I want to talk about is
special collections, I think, as part of the

00:31:47.159 --> 00:31:50.880
answer to the question of what do we do with
all that cool open space that we're no longer

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:56.769
going to lock down in the form of storage,
of shelving systems, and so forth. One thing

00:31:56.769 --> 00:32:02.179
we can think about are what are those collections
and sets of objects that truly speak of the

00:32:02.179 --> 00:32:07.760
place where they are located, that tell stories
about the nature of institution, about collecting

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:12.580
practices, about the communities that inhabit
a given location. And so I think the privilege,

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:17.669
the role of special collections, collections
that typically cut across categories, also

00:32:17.669 --> 00:32:23.659
from the world of objects, to the world of
documents and books, to every kind of conceivable

00:32:23.659 --> 00:32:30.450
imagined form of documentation of a process
or a practice-- those kinds of categories

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:35.010
and collections seem to me to offer special
opportunities. They tend to be buried. They

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:40.200
tend to be hard to get at. If we can bring
them out into spaces where they're open to

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:44.769
interaction, I think we're beginning to explore
this world of possibility that, moving away

00:32:44.769 --> 00:32:51.169
from a storage-based understanding of a library,
we're able to do.

00:32:51.169 --> 00:32:57.580
The next question I want to turn to is to
connections. That kind of storage answer to

00:32:57.580 --> 00:33:02.580
the question of the library, of course, tended
to emphasize collections as the centerpiece,

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:06.200
of the mission, of the identity of libraries.
And of course, collections are fundamental

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:12.419
to what libraries are, who they are, who they
serve, their histories, and so forth. But

00:33:12.419 --> 00:33:17.860
the collections were always the basis for
connections. And I think orienting our gaze

00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:22.990
towards the question of who we serve, how
we serve them, how we construct that spatial

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:28.600
platform as a platform that makes connections,
that served as a catalyst for connections,

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:34.990
is an essential part of this re-thinking process.
I've already anticipated, in a sense, with

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:38.769
respect to special collections-- I think I
see one of the opportunities that we have

00:33:38.769 --> 00:33:42.570
in this re-thinking and re-design process,
to really think about some of those kinds

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:47.679
of forms of selection and partitioning and
specialization that gave rise to separate

00:33:47.679 --> 00:33:53.929
institutions-- museum, the archive, the library.
Different kinds of museums, archives, and

00:33:53.929 --> 00:33:57.830
libraries. There are good reasons for those
separations, but there are also effects that

00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:05.640
those separations have that are in tension
with processes of discovery and knowledge

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:08.899
sharing.
And I think that one of the opportunities

00:34:08.899 --> 00:34:14.030
we have-- again, thinking about this, all
this space that we can now re-curate and re-design--

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:18.149
is thinking about how we create conversations
across media types, across different categories

00:34:18.149 --> 00:34:25.359
of objects. How we treat them, curate them,
tell stories with them. Interpret them in

00:34:25.359 --> 00:34:30.520
dialogue with one another, rather than separating
paper documents from three-dimensional objects

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:38.260
from artworks, and so on down the line. I
think the opportunity to think about what

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:44.079
a library is in terms of the categories of
objects that are on the shelves, the proliferation

00:34:44.079 --> 00:34:48.149
of maker spaces in libraries today, which
is a gesture in this direction, suggests that

00:34:48.149 --> 00:34:54.040
maybe tools, tools that allow us to learn,
to tell stories, to make arguments, ought

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:59.359
to be on the shelves alongside books. That
the kind of bookishness of the storage based

00:34:59.359 --> 00:35:04.380
model needs to be expanded to encompass other
kinds of forms of learning and knowledge that

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:10.310
involve the hand and not just the mind.
This was a concept that was come up with in

00:35:10.310 --> 00:35:16.210
a design studio that I was involved in-- the
GSD and my team at metaLAB-- [INAUDIBLE] a

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:20.780
couple of years. The notion of cool spots.
I'm sure all of you can remember the moment

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:25.140
when you used to walk around with your mobile
devices looking for hot spots, because hot

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:30.530
spot was like an oasis in the desert. You
were out seeking, you know, those three bars,

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:33.520
where you knew that actually had access to
the world.

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:38.480
Well, we now live in a world of ubiquitous
hot spots. The world has become a hot spot.

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:44.630
And the library has this special role as serving
as a platform of connection, but also of disconnection.

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:50.720
How do we want to design forms of disconnection?
It's a different way of framing the question

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:58.170
of the kind of ways in which libraries have
always served as places of refuge, as places

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:05.859
of contemplative remove from the everyday,
and yet, of engagement, re-engagement, from

00:36:05.859 --> 00:36:11.500
that perspective, of contemplative engagement.
So I think the question of how we design hot

00:36:11.500 --> 00:36:18.390
spots, the levels of disconnection that we
want buildings, in a sense, to create, enforce,

00:36:18.390 --> 00:36:22.380
to open up, I think, is a really rich possibility,
not to be underestimated.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:27.160
I've already anticipated this question of
moving between the hand and the mind, thinking

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:34.550
about the notion of tools as part of the set
of learning assets that libraries make available,

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:40.750
so I'm not going to dwell on that. I think
that the notion of librarians themselves,

00:36:40.750 --> 00:36:46.900
but also the communities that animate libraries,
as being engaged, in somehow a kind of expanded

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:52.349
sense of curatorial practices, where we use
the objects, whether they're documents, or

00:36:52.349 --> 00:36:58.320
they are collections of objects, to tell stories,
to share, to expose processes of learning

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:03.730
as they're happening, to solve community problems--
I think those are all opportunity spaces to

00:37:03.730 --> 00:37:10.730
really think about curatorial practice, the
curation of knowledge and culture as one of

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:15.410
the activities that we want to physically
facilitate through the design of spaces.

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:21.910
And I think, particularly, the re-design,
the re-use, the [FRENCH] of stack structures

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:28.810
to use as exhibition spaces strikes me as
an interesting hypothesis. I like that idea.

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:36.900
And that's we like to call in our group programmable
stacks. Imagine that stacks-- because we have

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:41.300
information systems, we have databases, we
no longer need to worry about books being

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:46.720
in an universal order. What if we can constantly
re-shuffle them, like a deck of cards, to

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:55.320
tell different kinds of stories? This is something
that is available to us as an option.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:38:01.290
Last but not least, I want to emphasize the
importance of process, of libraries as places

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:08.290
where we see learning taking place in a social
setting. One of the things that the space

00:38:08.290 --> 00:38:14.320
and the place do is really to create these
forms of visibility about processes that otherwise

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:19.420
pass unnoticed. I think this is particularly
important in environments like universities,

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:24.250
where there's so many amazing things always
happening, but it is so difficult to actually

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:28.849
be aware of them, to see them, to experience
them. I think the library has an opportunity

00:38:28.849 --> 00:38:34.750
to be that place.
And so, just to conclude, I want to come back

00:38:34.750 --> 00:38:40.410
to this notion of the library as an institution,
whose fundamental objective is really to perform,

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:44.839
to be a hybrid. Not to be a single thing,
not to be the place where knowledge or information

00:38:44.839 --> 00:38:52.310
is stored, but rather a place that's a hub,
that's a platform, a lab, a showcase, a switchboard,

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:57.640
somewhere you make connections. But above
all, a place that somehow has deep roots and

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:05.220
intersects deeply with all of the dynamics
that would grant meaning and activate the

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:15.150
potential for a physical structure to serve
as a catalyst within a community of learning.

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:21.560
Thank you.
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:44.910
Thank you. If I can appropriate your introduction,
I'll go right into mine, because there's so

00:39:44.910 --> 00:39:50.210
many themes that you bring up that are quite
resonant with the three case studies that

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:58.800
I'll try to elaborate on.
I was part of an MIT focus group on the future

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:05.390
of spaces of learning and the question of,
what is the function of the campus going to

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:12.329
become once there are all these online courses?
Why, if you can educate 50,000 people, would

00:40:12.329 --> 00:40:17.069
10,000 people come here? And of course, we've
lived through that theme over hundreds of

00:40:17.069 --> 00:40:22.849
years, with the notion of the book killing
architecture, or the building. And yet we

00:40:22.849 --> 00:40:27.890
know that certain things persist over time,
and there are good reasons for that.

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:34.099
We live in a time where the space of learning,
or the space of reading, or the library, is

00:40:34.099 --> 00:40:39.400
at once on our couch at home, it's by the
sea, it's on the train home. So we understand

00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:48.910
the ubiquity of our ability to tap into the
world. At the same time, the way that we blend

00:40:48.910 --> 00:40:55.990
low culture, high culture, personal space,
and public space has formed us in quite different

00:40:55.990 --> 00:41:02.040
ways. We no longer have to wait for Thursday
to watch that series. We can download them

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:06.920
at will.
And so one of the big questions that MIT asked

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:13.500
us was, why is the curriculum bundled in such
a way, when you can just take somebody else's

00:41:13.500 --> 00:41:19.960
bike, or somebody else's car? So the whole
notion of de-bundling as a conceptual idea

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:26.339
became a central theme to begin to think of,
how many other ways are there to learn? You

00:41:26.339 --> 00:41:31.790
learn by interning, you learn by researching,
you learn by extending your education to seven

00:41:31.790 --> 00:41:38.839
years or compressing it to three years. Nobody
said that four years is the right amount.

00:41:38.839 --> 00:41:46.000
We also know that place matters. There is
something about the aura, the sense of a place,

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:52.130
that is not replaceable, and that's what makes
UVA different than MIT. And somehow,

00:41:52.130 --> 00:41:59.930
we come to learn those things. At an organizational
level, we looked quickly at all sorts of teaching

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:07.210
spaces, learning spaces, top-down sort of
learning, and the ideological implications

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:12.319
that come with that.
The whole notion of the hardness and the idea

00:42:12.319 --> 00:42:20.760
that you're learning on a flat playing field.
The hierarchies are broken down. Spaces of

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:25.170
the TEAL that we have right here, where
you're at once connected to the world, but

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:31.750
connected to each other in a space of collaboration.
Our own Gershenfeld, and the idea that learning

00:42:31.750 --> 00:42:38.550
is done in a laboratory with many different
devices concurrent with each other. Or, you

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:44.940
know, the space of debate, as it were.
And yet, somehow, the space of architecture

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:54.780
is not yet defined. And we know the presence,
the aura, that that has. Think, if you will,

00:42:54.780 --> 00:43:01.250
about our own library here. Some years ago,
it was this dark and somehow dingy space.

00:43:01.250 --> 00:43:06.119
But with the opening of the oculus, that is
the one thing that opened up this pantheon,

00:43:06.119 --> 00:43:11.890
which has become yet a place again. So the
importance of architecture-- and I'm being

00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:17.510
opportunistic here, of course-- is that it
is central, in fact, to the cultivation of

00:43:17.510 --> 00:43:22.520
a campus, and in this case, the cultivation
of a library.

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:27.240
The two case studies I'll present to you very
quickly are the University of Melbourne, the

00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:33.880
Melbourne School of Design, and I'll end with
the RISD Library. In the context of the Melbourne

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:41.250
Uni, the building of a new school of architecture
and design planning became the opportunity

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:47.060
to break down the traditional boundaries between
what we call, or what we think of, design

00:43:47.060 --> 00:43:51.920
and the other disciplines on campus. Engineering,
over there, anthropology, over there. And

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:59.400
so the notion of the ground plane being opened
up and extending the public into the building,

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:04.349
essentially, giving over the entire base to
the entire campus, was something that was

00:44:04.349 --> 00:44:14.010
vital to making this the new center of the
university. At the same time, the idea of

00:44:14.010 --> 00:44:19.470
creating a new street that ran through there
enabled the possibility to create a triad

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:26.420
between the library, or the traditional library,
broken down, dissolved, the FabLab, which

00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:31.920
is just right above there, the space of making,
and the exhibition space.

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:37.230
Here, Tom Kvan, the dean of the school,
had this idea that places of research, places

00:44:37.230 --> 00:44:44.270
of thinking, need to find a way to externalize
that through the act of making. And making

00:44:44.270 --> 00:44:48.910
is a form of knowledge. You're producing a
form of knowledge. But what if you produce

00:44:48.910 --> 00:44:53.000
a form of knowledge and have no way to communicate
it to a broader public? And the exhibit space

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:58.730
becomes that opportunity.
So the building becomes a spatialization of

00:44:58.730 --> 00:45:06.520
this idea of the street that breaks down the
barriers between these spaces. These spaces,

00:45:06.520 --> 00:45:14.829
in turn, connect to the campus symbolically
and literally. And the actions that belong

00:45:14.829 --> 00:45:22.400
traditionally in this or that space leak out
into shared spaces, informal spaces. So if

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:26.740
spaces of the library making end up being
divided not by function, but by things like

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:33.240
noise, or smell, or privacy, or scale.
Other kinds of programmatic functions take

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:40.069
over. So the library effectively leaks up
into the building to the space, in fact, above

00:45:40.069 --> 00:45:44.930
the exhibition hall.
Now, the section of this building is quite

00:45:44.930 --> 00:45:52.010
important. Because by building a new piano nobile
for the building, we solved, in a way, two

00:45:52.010 --> 00:45:58.329
problems. A school of design dedicated to
about 2,000 designers without any dedicated

00:45:58.329 --> 00:46:03.910
space for design.
So what did that mean? It was a kind of trick

00:46:03.910 --> 00:46:09.520
to dovetail the spaces of scholarship with
the spaces of design and what are called hot

00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:17.650
spaces. Not hot as in wired, but hot as in
schedulable spaces, hot desks. What happens

00:46:17.650 --> 00:46:20.730
in the morning, what happens in the afternoon,
what happens in the evening.

00:46:20.730 --> 00:46:26.660
So effectively, the library moves up the building,
and the design moves down the building, effectively

00:46:26.660 --> 00:46:33.910
embracing each other. And the entire space,
what would become the traditional reading

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:41.480
room of a library, has now lifted up, gaining
natural light, and then the taxonomy of the

00:46:41.480 --> 00:46:48.220
building is impacted by it. What would have
been the traditional corridor is leaking systematically

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:55.150
on every floor into different spaces of design,
of research. And then different pieces of

00:46:55.150 --> 00:47:00.700
furniture begin to get associated with the
kinds of collaborations that you would do,

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:08.300
not only in design, but also in the library.
Collective works, individual works, drafting,

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:15.770
crit spaces, social spaces-- all of these
come together in a kind of sectional hybrid

00:47:15.770 --> 00:47:23.319
that identifies the importance of the intricate
configuration of the space with the larger,

00:47:23.319 --> 00:47:30.050
symbolic, and figural ambitions of identifying
the totemic importance of some of these spaces

00:47:30.050 --> 00:47:34.130
that need to be rightfully enclosed for acoustic reasons.

00:47:34.130 --> 00:47:41.690
And so we are not shy of what architecture
can do in this context, and are, you know,

00:47:41.690 --> 00:47:47.440
rightfully aware of the history that comes
with-- with these kinds of gestures.

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:54.180
Now, some years before this, a graduate, myself,
of the Rhode Island School of Design, we were

00:47:54.180 --> 00:48:02.579
asked to look at the hospital trust building,
the Fleet Bank at that time, and the entire

00:48:02.579 --> 00:48:08.150
building was being looked at for the new dormitories.
What they didn't know was what was going to

00:48:08.150 --> 00:48:15.190
happen with the banking hall.
Carol Terry, who had been already theorizing

00:48:15.190 --> 00:48:22.470
the future of the library some 30 years ago,
had a very clear sense of what this to become.

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:27.730
And it's really under her tutelage that we
entered into this discussion in any sense.

00:48:27.730 --> 00:48:35.339
And so the idea that this vast and heavy monolith
of dormitories would be riding over the library

00:48:35.339 --> 00:48:40.020
would mean effectively that this is their
living room. This is their lounge. This is

00:48:40.020 --> 00:48:48.490
their social space.
Also something quite ironic was this idea

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:55.099
that Carol brought to us. In the previous
library, ironically, the students had no access

00:48:55.099 --> 00:49:01.109
to books. What did she want to do? She wanted
to make a browsing library, to make books

00:49:01.109 --> 00:49:06.349
accessible once again. Knowing fully well
that they see everything on their screens,

00:49:06.349 --> 00:49:11.970
their iPhones, and laptops, she wanted a space
where the word and the image come together.

00:49:11.970 --> 00:49:18.720
And that has something to do with the library
of a school of art, the visual arts. Knowing

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:25.420
that knowledge is internalized in very different
ways, we look at the picture first, then we

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:32.290
read the words, and that's how knowledge begins
to happen. That's how you light the fire.

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:38.690
Now, in the banking hall, it turns out that
what she had in mind was, you know, about

00:49:38.690 --> 00:49:43.800
double the amount of programs, so it could
not have been fulfilled in that, in the existing

00:49:43.800 --> 00:49:52.030
square footage. So building new structures
was a kind of indispensable aspect of being

00:49:52.030 --> 00:49:59.290
able to pull this project off. And because
of purely technical reasons, code, reasons,

00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:06.420
we needed not only to make the mezzanine accessible
with a new elevator, but also those spaces

00:50:06.420 --> 00:50:13.530
that would need to-- otherwise not be able
to fit. The spaces of video watching, spaces

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:19.520
of studying that would go on top of that,
making them accessible meant that we needed

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:25.960
bridges, and stairs, and things that would
ultimately transform this library into something

00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:31.309
else beyond the living room.
The staircase that connects them, the main

00:50:31.309 --> 00:50:40.660
plaza, the central space that brings the campus
together, is also its main theater. That theater

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:47.200
is the living room of the campus, in one sense,
but then there are many other types of reading

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:53.410
areas, research areas, spaces of intercollaboration
and interaction around it. But just to imagine

00:50:53.410 --> 00:50:59.730
what happens in this space in the abstract,
over time we came to understand that theater,

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:06.800
which was never something significant at RISD,
began to have a life of its own on campus.

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:17.020
Weddings. Music. And of course, arts. The
space of drawing. That this becomes a theater

00:51:17.020 --> 00:51:25.710
for drawing is an essential part and extension
of the-- of the-- nature lab of the Rhode

00:51:25.710 --> 00:51:31.480
Island School of Design. So important to its
foundation program. So the library is everything

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:37.829
but the space of the book, and at the same
time, Carol was able to get over 80% of the

00:51:37.829 --> 00:51:46.349
books, all as the insular wrap around this
main public space. I'll end, then, with this

00:51:46.349 --> 00:51:53.990
idea of flexibility. On the one hand, the
notion of spaces today under huge economic

00:51:53.990 --> 00:52:01.099
duress, need to be absolutely flexible. And
you-- you must imagine that they will certainly

00:52:01.099 --> 00:52:08.089
become something else in 10 years, in 30 years.
So the idea of the-- the programmable floor,

00:52:08.089 --> 00:52:15.720
or the programmable ceiling, but free of any
definitive program, is important. But also,

00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:22.510
the notion that some notions about flexibility
have to do with something that is irreducible--

00:52:22.510 --> 00:52:27.420
the scale of the individual, the scale of
five people working together, the dark space,

00:52:27.420 --> 00:52:32.980
the light space, the acoustic space, and one
has to be mindful of those.

00:52:32.980 --> 00:52:41.960
But above all, the idea that the audience
that you're serving is a public. And is a

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:49.359
public that extends the field of the urban
realm into these buildings, and makes them

00:52:49.359 --> 00:52:55.480
culturally vital. And that was one of the
missions that Carol sought to bring into this

00:52:55.480 --> 00:53:01.119
building, and she did. This is not the RISD
library. In fact, it invites the entire city

00:53:01.119 --> 00:53:17.420
of Providence, and beyond, to come inside.
Thank you.

00:53:17.420 --> 00:53:23.059
[APPLAUSE]
Hello. I'm very pleased to be with you here

00:53:23.059 --> 00:53:28.599
this evening. You will not see pretty pictures
from me. You've seen pictures of libraries

00:53:28.599 --> 00:53:34.839
that I had something to do with already. I'm
the representative of the public library.

00:53:34.839 --> 00:53:40.800
And I'm going to talk to you a bit about public
libraries. You'll hear many of the same comments

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:48.599
and words that you've heard from the three
previous speakers as well. And I promise to

00:53:48.599 --> 00:53:54.550
close with one of my favorite library stories
at the moment when I learn that my time is

00:53:54.550 --> 00:54:00.890
nearly up. So you can have that to look forward
to. Anna gave us a few questions to respond

00:54:00.890 --> 00:54:05.990
to, and I'm going to do that here as well.
First, for those of you who don't know about

00:54:05.990 --> 00:54:12.150
public libraries, they really are the magic
places in the world. They were for me when

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:19.369
I was a child. My guess is many of you would
have similar stories to tell. Public libraries

00:54:19.369 --> 00:54:26.720
are funded locally. More than 90%, in some
cases, as much as 98% of the money, is approved

00:54:26.720 --> 00:54:33.869
by those local people elected to make the
hard decisions about where tax money goes

00:54:33.869 --> 00:54:39.579
on a very local basis. So they're very different
from one another, and each very responsive

00:54:39.579 --> 00:54:45.290
to its own community. At the same time, public
libraries are more alike one another than

00:54:45.290 --> 00:54:50.920
they are different from one another, and that's
part of the way that the-- that the magic

00:54:50.920 --> 00:54:55.049
happens.
And yes, as you've heard others say, we are

00:54:55.049 --> 00:55:02.030
not about storage any more. We are, in fact,
about how to make the space that the community

00:55:02.030 --> 00:55:09.569
needs at that particular time and in that
particular place. And our colleagues and partners

00:55:09.569 --> 00:55:16.369
in doing so are often architects. One of the
questions was, what is the role of architecture

00:55:16.369 --> 00:55:21.040
in determining the future of the library?
You know, you saw some of those wonderful

00:55:21.040 --> 00:55:26.240
images from the previous speakers of what
public libraries are. One of the things that

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:33.030
happens with a new building or a newly designed
space is everybody pays attention in a different

00:55:33.030 --> 00:55:38.890
way than they did before. You walk in expecting
it to be different than it was before because

00:55:38.890 --> 00:55:43.690
it looks different. And it gives you that
as a way of doing it. If architecture did

00:55:43.690 --> 00:55:48.809
nothing else, other than give people the opportunity
to change the way they look at something and

00:55:48.809 --> 00:55:54.530
think of it, it would serve a wonderful purpose
for all of us.

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:59.859
And public libraries, that is ever true. In
public libraries in local communities, there

00:55:59.859 --> 00:56:05.480
are people who have the stories that I said
was mine, that the library was the magic place

00:56:05.480 --> 00:56:12.299
I found out about the world, and many, many
other stories. And often, there are people

00:56:12.299 --> 00:56:19.319
who want those libraries to stay exactly as
they have been. That library of their imagination,

00:56:19.319 --> 00:56:26.049
of their past as they remember it now. So
architecture helps to say, there is something

00:56:26.049 --> 00:56:31.630
a little different going on here, in that
exact way.

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:36.690
One of the challenges of public libraries
is because we do belong to the public, that

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:41.680
means everybody has a say in what it's going
to be, and has an opportunity to critique

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:46.270
what you're going to do with the building
that's coming and with the services that are

00:56:46.270 --> 00:56:53.230
there. It's kind of David Adjaye not to tell
the story of the woman in one of the communities

00:56:53.230 --> 00:56:58.849
that he showed the library of, who stood up
at a public meeting and said she had been

00:56:58.849 --> 00:57:04.079
trying to call England and talk to Prince
Charles, because she heard he didn't like

00:57:04.079 --> 00:57:08.630
David's buildings, and she didn't like it
either. And she wanted to see how that could

00:57:08.630 --> 00:57:16.430
happen together. True story, yes, David? Right.
She's become a fan of the building, finally,

00:57:16.430 --> 00:57:21.950
but really wanted it to be in red brick, and
just like her house, and just like her neighborhood.

00:57:21.950 --> 00:57:26.319
And was quite sure that what we were talking
about wasn't going to fit, and wasn't going

00:57:26.319 --> 00:57:32.329
to work for her in her neighborhood.
But in fact, the role that libraries play

00:57:32.329 --> 00:57:38.859
in local communities is really magic. For
many people who are disconnected with the

00:57:38.859 --> 00:57:45.900
government that is in fact theirs, it is the
way that that government says, this is what

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:53.059
you are worth. This beautiful building, with
all of these magic things inside, to open

00:57:53.059 --> 00:57:59.230
up the world to you, and the place is for
you to be, this is yours. And it's your government

00:57:59.230 --> 00:58:06.369
that has created and provided that for you.
It really is a wonderful thing to be involved

00:58:06.369 --> 00:58:11.400
in.
So in fact, architects and librarians are

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:19.319
allies as we move forward together in this
brave new world of what libraries become.

00:58:19.319 --> 00:58:26.079
And they are changing. They used to be about
collections, as you've heard from others.

00:58:26.079 --> 00:58:33.940
We are now, still, about connections in a
way that makes sense, and helps people find

00:58:33.940 --> 00:58:38.900
a way to connect with everybody in their community
in one way or another, and with that community

00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:44.180
and that local government, very often. That
civic engagement is an important role for

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:48.660
libraries, and it's something that in public
libraries, we pay a great deal of attention

00:58:48.660 --> 00:58:54.140
to.
It also becomes increasingly a place for collaboration.

00:58:54.140 --> 00:58:58.569
You saw that with the RISD library, and the
ways-- and the Melbourne project, and the

00:58:58.569 --> 00:59:03.799
way that different disciplines worked together
and were a part of what it is that was being

00:59:03.799 --> 00:59:10.190
done in that building. And collaborations,
or collaborating, is a big part of what libraries

00:59:10.190 --> 00:59:15.530
do.
And we are moving increasingly into being

00:59:15.530 --> 00:59:25.339
places for creation. Now, it is usual for
people to come to the library to create a

00:59:25.339 --> 00:59:29.380
report for a group of sixth graders that are
going to go to the library study rooms to

00:59:29.380 --> 00:59:36.660
work together, or a dance project that somebody
wants to create. And the library's a great

00:59:36.660 --> 00:59:41.930
place to meet a musician you don't know, and
try to figure out how to make that happen.

00:59:41.930 --> 00:59:47.700
Or to learn how to read. Nothing says, I don't
really need literacy training than walking

00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:52.829
into a public library, and that's a common
place for that to happen, too.

00:59:52.829 --> 00:59:56.730
So now, I'm told it's nearly the end of my
time, and I get to tell you what is one of

00:59:56.730 --> 01:00:04.040
my favorite library stories. And I think it
talks about what the library can do and does

01:00:04.040 --> 01:00:11.819
do. A number of years ago, in a library where
I used to work, a very, very talented children's

01:00:11.819 --> 01:00:17.950
librarian came to me with an idea. She was
trying to figure out how we could help our

01:00:17.950 --> 01:00:25.799
community teach children to read, when so
many start kindergarten behind, and stay behind

01:00:25.799 --> 01:00:31.329
as they move forward. Her great idea became
a program that I've used at very nearly every

01:00:31.329 --> 01:00:36.910
library where I have worked.
And in this program, books, usually paperback

01:00:36.910 --> 01:00:43.599
books, usually ones that are not worth a great
deal of money and that we got quite inexpensively,

01:00:43.599 --> 01:00:49.450
would go to the library, to the classroom.
And a volunteer would go to that classroom

01:00:49.450 --> 01:00:56.020
every three weeks. We targeted classrooms
where the reading level was not at grade level,

01:00:56.020 --> 01:01:01.309
and where these kids needed to learn the magic
of reading, and certainly didn't have that

01:01:01.309 --> 01:01:06.920
sense about this very hard-to-do thing. So
the volunteers-- sometimes staff, sometimes

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:13.079
volunteers-- would go and they would book
talk. In the library world, think of it as

01:01:13.079 --> 01:01:18.180
the previews of coming attractions in movies.
It's where you tell enough about the book

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:22.980
so that this eight-year-old or nine-year-old
or ten-year-old wants to get their hands on

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:27.190
that book. And because-- and when they're
done with the book, they can pass it on to

01:01:27.190 --> 01:01:32.030
somebody else, bring it back, whatever. We
don't care. It's to get them to have that

01:01:32.030 --> 01:01:36.490
book in their hand, and excited about opening
that book, that is the real point of what

01:01:36.490 --> 01:01:40.859
you're doing.
So one of the people that worked on this program

01:01:40.859 --> 01:01:46.579
told this story. She told about being in a
classroom. It was a fourth-grade classroom.

01:01:46.579 --> 01:01:52.309
And in that classroom, there were four languages
spoken in addition to English, so she had

01:01:52.309 --> 01:01:57.049
three language interpreters who were interpreting
her words for the fourth-graders in that class

01:01:57.049 --> 01:02:03.160
that were transitioning to English. And at
the end of her program, as often happens when

01:02:03.160 --> 01:02:07.920
you do that kind of presentation, all the
kids rushed up and wanted to tell her things.

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:13.859
You know, I love that book about the dog!
You know, I love dogs! My cousin has a dog!

01:02:13.859 --> 01:02:18.710
You know, all of that kind of chatter.
And during all of this time, there was a little

01:02:18.710 --> 01:02:26.140
boy who was standing right beside her. He
didn't say anything. And after all the kids

01:02:26.140 --> 01:02:32.049
had had their say, Kathy leaned over and she
said, did you have something you wanted to

01:02:32.049 --> 01:02:38.780
say? And he put his hand in his pocket, and
he reached out his hand, and in it was his

01:02:38.780 --> 01:02:47.809
library card. And he said, I American now.
Now he wasn't, of course, but he was, in all

01:02:47.809 --> 01:02:53.089
the ways that are wonderful. And whatever
the library has in the future, I know that

01:02:53.089 --> 01:03:06.420
that will continue to be an ingredient of
it. Thank you.

01:03:06.420 --> 01:03:09.950
[APPLAUSE]
So I'm not sure how I'm supposed to follow

01:03:09.950 --> 01:03:17.059
that story, but I'm going to try. And when
I first thought about what I wanted to say

01:03:17.059 --> 01:03:23.349
tonight, I wanted to talk about what a remarkable
time it is to be a librarian. And it really

01:03:23.349 --> 01:03:29.880
is. I mean, we can take nothing for granted
right now in libraries. And it's-- that's

01:03:29.880 --> 01:03:36.720
kind of remarkable. The challenge in trying
to understand what a library as a space would

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:43.030
look like, should look like, should do when,
as Jeffrey says, we can carry much of human

01:03:43.030 --> 01:03:48.710
knowledge in our pocket, that's a really cool
challenge. And so, you know, I feel a great

01:03:48.710 --> 01:03:54.829
privilege to be in this profession right now.
That's really cool.

01:03:54.829 --> 01:03:58.829
But at 10:30 this morning, I had an experience.
I had this experience a lot in the year I've

01:03:58.829 --> 01:04:03.589
been here, but I had this experience where
it's felt like a really remarkable time to

01:04:03.589 --> 01:04:09.220
be at MIT. So for those of you who didn't
hear the news at 10:30 this morning, MIT announced

01:04:09.220 --> 01:04:16.490
that a group of scientists at MIT, along with
the collaborators from Caltech, detected,

01:04:16.490 --> 01:04:22.420
for the first time, gravitational waves. Which
I don't really understand what they are, but

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:27.880
I understand-- but what I know is that they
are ripples in the time-space continuum that

01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:33.059
were predicted by Einstein in 1916. And at
the time, he predicted that we'd never be

01:04:33.059 --> 01:04:40.829
able to really see them or detect them. And
after years and years, and many, many collaborators,

01:04:40.829 --> 01:04:46.339
we detected them.
And in the article about that, one of the

01:04:46.339 --> 01:04:50.240
scientists on the team talked about it being
a very visceral experience, that they could

01:04:50.240 --> 01:04:57.180
actually hear this moment when two black holes
merged together and collided to create another

01:04:57.180 --> 01:05:05.160
black hole. And so there's something there,
right, about a sensory experience. That in

01:05:05.160 --> 01:05:09.510
this thing that is a kind of science, that
is for those who are not scientists sort of

01:05:09.510 --> 01:05:15.819
beyond our comprehension, that is still very
visceral and sensory. And I think there's

01:05:15.819 --> 01:05:22.270
some things there to be played with around
library as space, and around the question

01:05:22.270 --> 01:05:26.760
that Jeffrey raises about, sort of, what we
do with this space that we can free up with

01:05:26.760 --> 01:05:33.130
collections? And I think the question before
that is, well, which collections should we

01:05:33.130 --> 01:05:42.420
keep, and why? And maybe they're the ones
where they help us have these sensory experiences.

01:05:42.420 --> 01:05:48.280
There's a couple of other library pieces to
this gravitational waves story which I'm still

01:05:48.280 --> 01:05:55.569
fascinated by. One of things that was really
wonderful about it is-- I think we all at

01:05:55.569 --> 01:06:01.609
MIT, or everyone I ran into today at MIT,
felt like they were a part of it. Right? We

01:06:01.609 --> 01:06:06.700
were all-- I walked a little taller today.
I mean-- I had nothing to do with this, I

01:06:06.700 --> 01:06:14.180
had never even heard-- but I feel pretty darn
good to be at MIT today. It's that connection.

01:06:14.180 --> 01:06:20.400
I feel connected to research that happened
here. Because I'm here, because I'm part of

01:06:20.400 --> 01:06:28.880
this community. And I think, again, libraries
can facilitate that, can enhance that.

01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:35.450
Another interesting library part of that story
is that within about 20 minutes of the announcement

01:06:35.450 --> 01:06:42.579
that came out, the website that hosts the
article that describes the research behind

01:06:42.579 --> 01:06:51.710
this was having some connection problems.
Right? But-- people wanted to read the research.

01:06:51.710 --> 01:06:57.559
And they were-- you know, thousands of people
were hitting that website, the Physical Review

01:06:57.559 --> 01:07:03.000
Letters of the American Physics Society, and
there were some connection problems.

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:09.230
OK-- what's really cool about that is librarians
at MIT were scrambling to try and figure out

01:07:09.230 --> 01:07:14.180
how to make sure that folks here could still
have access to that research. And literally

01:07:14.180 --> 01:07:19.880
as I'm speaking, some of the rockstar librarians
that work here, and the staff and the people

01:07:19.880 --> 01:07:27.369
who work here at the MIT libraries, are curating
a collection of articles that are the foundational

01:07:27.369 --> 01:07:35.539
research that led up to this huge breakthrough
in science. They're curating a collection

01:07:35.539 --> 01:07:42.410
of articles that are available in D space,
which is our open-access repository. So these

01:07:42.410 --> 01:07:48.480
will be articles that are open to the world
to read. This is our way of saying, we do

01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:54.670
this stuff at MIT, and we're sharing it. Who
is our community? Our community is anyone

01:07:54.670 --> 01:08:01.289
who wants to understand this amazing breakthrough
that happened here at MIT today. Right? We

01:08:01.289 --> 01:08:07.750
have a big community. And we're connected,
and we want to be that place, whether it's

01:08:07.750 --> 01:08:13.730
in-- digitally or physically, we want to be
that place of connection to each other, and

01:08:13.730 --> 01:08:20.609
to science, and to learning, and to possibility.
You know, last night, David talked about magic

01:08:20.609 --> 01:08:23.730
here. So we've talked about connection, and
we've talked about magic. And last night,

01:08:23.730 --> 01:08:28.779
David said something about how his-- and I
hope I get this right-- so that he sees part

01:08:28.779 --> 01:08:35.190
of his job as to sort of cast a spell with
the work that he does to make people think

01:08:35.190 --> 01:08:42.259
something is important. And I want to extend
that. It just was such a lovely way of talking

01:08:42.259 --> 01:08:50.279
about how space can do that, how places and
physicality can do that, can communicate that.

01:08:50.279 --> 01:08:54.679
For me, what I want-- I want libraries to
do that, want spaces to do that. I also want

01:08:54.679 --> 01:09:02.359
them to be-- to communicate and to be places
where people know that they are important,

01:09:02.359 --> 01:09:10.609
and that they belong here. I want the libraries
to be that space where the students and the

01:09:10.609 --> 01:09:17.859
community can feel that that-- those riches,
and that the science that happens here, the

01:09:17.859 --> 01:09:21.790
learning that happens here, the riches-- that
they are a part of that, the way that I felt

01:09:21.790 --> 01:09:28.310
a part of the announcement this morning. I
want the libraries to be that space where

01:09:28.310 --> 01:09:35.130
you can-- it communicates to the community
all of the science that's happening today,

01:09:35.130 --> 01:09:40.940
all that has happened previously, here and
elsewhere, that they have access to it, that

01:09:40.940 --> 01:09:47.679
they're a part of it. Similar to the experience
that the young child described, I'm American

01:09:47.679 --> 01:09:55.020
now-- in a research library, I'm a scholar
now. I'm a scholar now. And I get to be in

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:59.290
this place.
And we used to feel that, I think. You know,

01:09:59.290 --> 01:10:05.710
I-- I told a story last night, and I'll tell
it again today. I'm a first-generation college

01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:11.210
student, so when I walked into the library
at Duke University, to Perkins Library, too

01:10:11.210 --> 01:10:18.650
many years ago, for the first time-- to see
those books, to see those stacks of books,

01:10:18.650 --> 01:10:27.360
and to feel like, I get to read these. These
are for me too, now. That's an amazing experience.

01:10:27.360 --> 01:10:32.940
And is it just books that can do that? I think
not. I think it could be really interesting,

01:10:32.940 --> 01:10:39.080
and is really interesting to think about libraries
as spaces-- digital, physical spaces, cultural

01:10:39.080 --> 01:10:46.170
spaces, metaphorical spaces, where people
feel that they belong in the scholarly conversation.

01:10:46.170 --> 01:10:49.610
And that's, you know, what I hope we'll be
able to do here at MIT, what I know we'll

01:10:49.610 --> 01:11:14.320
be able to do here at MIT. So, thanks very much. [APPLAUSE]

01:11:14.340 --> 01:11:43.090
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

01:11:43.090 --> 01:11:52.590
I think we covered magic. I was going to open with magic. But I really do
feel like there's enough ways that we've entered

01:11:52.590 --> 01:11:58.590
the topic already. And maybe the way in which
architecture serves it will come back. I will

01:11:58.590 --> 01:12:04.070
ensure that to some extent. So maybe
let's go to the next big topic, the future.

01:12:04.070 --> 01:12:09.280
I mean, obviously you've also all addressed
that to some extent. So maybe we're going

01:12:09.280 --> 01:12:18.380
to be repeating some of the things, but maybe
slightly differently formulated or in conversation now.

01:12:18.380 --> 01:12:26.130
And it's a key topic tonight. It's on our
poster. And David Adjaye will participate

01:12:26.130 --> 01:12:33.920
in every event. If you will participate this
semester with us, future will be on the table.

01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:43.690
In a way, MIT has copyrighted the word. But
it's also always on architects minds. And

01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:48.530
it's, in a way, part of the job description
of the design process. So even when one thinks

01:12:48.530 --> 01:12:53.820
about historical continuum as an architect,
they are really kind of thinking in the prospective

01:12:53.820 --> 01:12:58.280
mode.
But if we can begin to sort of pry it open

01:12:58.280 --> 01:13:04.050
and slice it, I actually want to go to something
that Jeffrey didn't say today,

01:13:04.050 --> 01:13:13.440
but has said in his work on the library after
the book, in a way, and I'll paraphrase. He

01:13:13.440 --> 01:13:19.010
said something that if in the past the library's
main task was to respond to society's scarcity

01:13:19.010 --> 01:13:25.460
of information, it's contemporary mandate
is to deal with its opposite-- the abundance

01:13:25.460 --> 01:13:30.350
of information.
And then from the other angle, David Adjaye

01:13:30.350 --> 01:13:35.480
has said that, in a way, I'm not taking about
the library specifically, that we need a new

01:13:35.480 --> 01:13:42.620
interpretation of civicness. And that on top
of architectural terms, on top of maybe needing

01:13:42.620 --> 01:13:50.330
to edify, we need that civicness to also be
production of a hybrid space with multiple

01:13:50.330 --> 01:13:58.290
signals, multiple entries, multiple cultural
references, and that heterogeneity of meaning.

01:13:58.290 --> 01:14:08.290
And so it seems like future is more and more
hybrid. And so I am offering to that to you

01:14:08.290 --> 01:14:17.880
collectively to enter from various angles.
Well, maybe one starting point, just to embroider

01:14:17.880 --> 01:14:25.330
a little bit further on this question of managing
the overabundance of information, which, by

01:14:25.330 --> 01:14:31.989
the way, as my colleague Ann Blair has
shown in some of her work, was a problem that

01:14:31.989 --> 01:14:36.610
was acutely felt very early in the history
of printing. I mean, in the 16th century people

01:14:36.610 --> 01:14:41.810
were in an absolute panic about the overabundance
of print matter. Imagine how they would have

01:14:41.810 --> 01:14:43.550
felt today.
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:43.550 --> 01:14:53.460
They didn't even have even an nth percentage
of even the redundancy of information channels that we have today.

01:14:53.460 --> 01:14:58.780
But I think at every moment of that challenge,
one of the things that keeps shifting and

01:14:58.780 --> 01:15:03.719
that's really rich, think we'll come back
to your question about the future, what's

01:15:03.719 --> 01:15:12.300
emerging as a new set of challenges. There's
a different kind of cognitive sort of style

01:15:12.300 --> 01:15:18.000
that characterizes these shifts in media and
information and ways of interacting and so

01:15:18.000 --> 01:15:22.340
forth.
I think designing spaces for-- we're still

01:15:22.340 --> 01:15:27.270
at the beginning of the digital revolution.
It's hard to remember that. We have this sense

01:15:27.270 --> 01:15:31.760
of being kind of overwhelmed by all the transformations.
But those transformations have barely gelled

01:15:31.760 --> 01:15:37.179
in new knowledge forms, and new cultural forms,
and new social forms.

01:15:37.179 --> 01:15:42.659
So I think it is an incredibly challenging
task. But what we do see is a new kind of

01:15:42.659 --> 01:15:48.090
hybridity where things are being realigned.
And the privilege that certain forms once

01:15:48.090 --> 01:15:56.070
enjoyed-- like the print record-- those privileges
no longer exist in that same stable relationship

01:15:56.070 --> 01:16:04.909
to other forms, other channels and so forth.
And for me, because I love to beat up on computer

01:16:04.909 --> 01:16:10.000
work rooms because they seem to be
the-- not only really dumb things but they

01:16:10.000 --> 01:16:16.489
really crystallized the central problem which is weaving together information in space in ways that add value

01:16:16.489 --> 01:16:24.159
rather than simply being functional sort of
post-factum legacy solutions.

01:16:24.159 --> 01:16:29.440
We talk a lot about smart spaces. If there
was ever an institution that's associated

01:16:29.440 --> 01:16:34.860
with the notion of smart spaces, it's the
library. But we really don't have good answers

01:16:34.860 --> 01:16:41.120
when it comes to the way electronic media
intersect with these other cognitive apps

01:16:41.120 --> 01:16:45.630
which are essential to our experience-- the
way we share knowledge, the way we perform

01:16:45.630 --> 01:16:52.150
knowledge. And I think it's really an exciting
frontier to be at. Even if it's a challenging one.

01:16:57.400 --> 01:17:01.300
Do you want to take on the civic, or you know maybe the

01:17:01.310 --> 01:17:17.190
way in which you summon public, or represent it in the building. Either or, I think one is more active than the other.
No, I think this all, the technology

01:17:17.190 --> 01:17:28.580
offers to that sort of idea that we understand of it, as this kind of repository of shelving systems, storage, that's kind of open storage that's on display that we so enjoyed and romance

01:17:28.580 --> 01:17:38.239
about. And that removal from architecture of that primary idea of the stack in the reading where it

01:17:38.239 --> 01:17:44.239
became the kind of two sort of tropes.
I'm going to defend it in a second, but, yeah.

01:17:44.239 --> 01:17:49.690
I love it.
And I can go there with two archetypes. In

01:17:49.690 --> 01:18:01.480
the system with a certain sort of redundancy
and that as the kind of primary agency in

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:07.190
it's-- I think in a research library, it's
a very different thing. When I'm talking about

01:18:07.190 --> 01:18:11.280
a public library in a community, it becomes
something else.

01:18:11.280 --> 01:18:16.070
Because in a way the hybridity has a civic
quality that we're discussing here in a library

01:18:16.070 --> 01:18:21.800
becomes about a way in which our society is
modernizing and identifying and the way in

01:18:21.800 --> 01:18:28.380
which the library becomes a kind of device
that becomes a kind of respite or a glomerating

01:18:28.380 --> 01:18:37.130
set of activities that start to make sense
to the sort of the modernity that's happening

01:18:37.130 --> 01:18:43.390
around us. Shall I say that?
So in a way, the public library has sot of

01:18:43.390 --> 01:18:50.820
become a kind of space that is starting to
kind of take on more of these-- not just mind-issues,

01:18:50.820 --> 01:18:58.690
but also body-issues, so it's oscillating between
mind and body as a kind of a space

01:18:58.690 --> 01:19:06.820
that is serving communities specifically
away from the commercial agent-- the activities of commercial

01:19:06.820 --> 01:19:11.440
space.
Now, a research library is a very different

01:19:11.440 --> 01:19:16.370
phenomena. You are talking about an academic
environment. So you have this idea of the

01:19:16.370 --> 01:19:24.239
stack and the romance of the stack, and the
need to kind of ritualize the reading room,

01:19:24.239 --> 01:19:29.650
the place where the great minds is in, it's
powerful. It's your artifact. It's the thing

01:19:29.650 --> 01:19:33.420
that you want to kind of enjoy.
And you want to create a kind of simulation

01:19:33.420 --> 01:19:42.530
of that continually. But the question then
is, even for me, the moment when you can capture

01:19:42.530 --> 01:19:48.750
that idea becomes Disneyfied. And it just
becomes a caricature of itself as a way to

01:19:48.750 --> 01:19:53.840
stage the notion of the environment where
the authenticity's no longer real, but it's

01:19:53.840 --> 01:20:00.630
a kind of device to send out.
I agree with you, as an image.

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:05.850
Hybridity is a reality.
This in a way for me was the problem

01:20:05.850 --> 01:20:12.690
of the library, of its future, as it's bracketed
by these two notions. On one end, the kind

01:20:12.690 --> 01:20:17.790
of amassing and hybridization of the media
in which knowledge is produced, and on the

01:20:17.790 --> 01:20:24.000
other, the actual hybridity of, and the need
for, different kinds of references and openings

01:20:24.000 --> 01:20:30.010
for a kind of public that is no longer a monolithic
public. Seems like kind of an interesting

01:20:30.010 --> 01:20:35.130
question. But I think you sort of take this on differently.

01:20:35.130 --> 01:20:43.360
For me, it's an exciting time for me. Because
all of these discussions trigger an understanding

01:20:43.360 --> 01:20:50.580
of the discipline of architecture. Which can
be less apologetic than it was maybe 10 or

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:55.560
20 years ago. And so--
I think that's shared at this table.

01:20:55.560 --> 01:21:02.260
So the idea that you could produce not only
a form of knowledge, but a form of consciousness

01:21:02.260 --> 01:21:12.570
through making a subject through the public.
So for me, if you acknowledge that the civic

01:21:12.570 --> 01:21:18.800
or the public isn't monolithic, the language that
is being put, materializes in the way you

01:21:18.800 --> 01:21:21.800
specialize things, the way that you organize
them.

01:21:21.800 --> 01:21:29.570
It's all part of that process not so much
of mirroring society, but potentially projecting

01:21:29.570 --> 01:21:39.530
it in a way that it has never been before.
So the idea of speculation becomes associated

01:21:39.530 --> 01:21:48.960
with a positive way of imagining a future,
something that we are always having to be

01:21:48.960 --> 01:21:56.949
very apologetic about.
We don't have to talk about this.

01:21:56.949 --> 01:22:02.940
Do you want to talk about your two futures?
The zoom in and the zoom out?

01:22:02.940 --> 01:22:11.360
Last night when we had the discussion with the
library task force, and our challenge was

01:22:11.360 --> 01:22:16.020
to talk to them about how to plan for the
future. I said that I had found it helpful

01:22:16.020 --> 01:22:22.810
to think about two futures. The first is what
you can probably figure out is gonna happen.

01:22:22.810 --> 01:22:29.889
So we can see a little bit ahead and you're
absolutely right, it's not anywhere near the

01:22:29.889 --> 01:22:34.650
midpoint of this digital revolution.
We won't be carrying around things that sometime

01:22:34.650 --> 01:22:41.540
we can probably see. We certainly won't need
to plug things in. Maybe we won't even need

01:22:41.540 --> 01:22:48.440
to charge them, if we're really lucky. So
we can sort of see that world coming. And

01:22:48.440 --> 01:22:53.080
so we have to do the immediate planning for
exactly what that is.

01:22:53.080 --> 01:22:58.610
And then what's really fun is to separate
from that the really far-out wild future that

01:22:58.610 --> 01:23:07.860
you have no idea of, what might happen-- dare
I give the example I gave last night? I was

01:23:07.860 --> 01:23:14.530
at a library program where I heard someone
say, you know we'll soon, within a century

01:23:14.530 --> 01:23:19.670
or two, be at the place where people don't
read. Where they don't have to understand

01:23:19.670 --> 01:23:25.270
what words mean. It'll all be spoken to them.
Or maybe it's given to them in some other

01:23:25.270 --> 01:23:30.820
way we can't imagine. You take a pill and
you know it all, or something.

01:23:30.820 --> 01:23:36.190
But when you think about the fact that there
once wasn't reading-- until there was printing,

01:23:36.190 --> 01:23:41.550
there was no need for reading. And when you
think about my mathematics work with slide

01:23:41.550 --> 01:23:47.440
rules, do kids know what slide rules are anymore?
The world does change, and tools change, and

01:23:47.440 --> 01:23:51.679
maybe that's an amazing one, too. So what
would a library be then?

01:23:51.679 --> 01:23:58.370
So I think what's interesting to me is to
think about libraries in terms of, as you

01:23:58.370 --> 01:24:06.340
said, what's the future that we kind of know
is coming? What's the wild crazy future, right?

01:24:06.340 --> 01:24:09.239
But also, what's the future we want?
Yes.

01:24:09.239 --> 01:24:15.139
Right? What's the future we want for scholarly
communication, right? Do we want, in fact,

01:24:15.139 --> 01:24:23.250
a scholarly communication system that gets
around some of the real brokenness of it now

01:24:23.250 --> 01:24:28.880
where one of the biggest breakthroughs of
our generation in science is behind a pay-wall,

01:24:28.880 --> 01:24:33.770
right?
Do we want a more open system where that knowledge

01:24:33.770 --> 01:24:39.560
is truly available, or truly taking advantage
of the technology where that knowledge could

01:24:39.560 --> 01:24:46.739
be in anyone's pocket? If we want that, then
what does that imply for what libraries then

01:24:46.739 --> 01:24:52.150
do? Because they used to manage scarcity,
not just in physical form, but also now we're

01:24:52.150 --> 01:24:58.489
managing scarcity based on this very broken
system of scholarly communication.

01:24:58.489 --> 01:25:06.600
OK, I have a question to the architects on
the panel. I have another. I really do want

01:25:06.600 --> 01:25:14.130
to talk about stacks because as this goes
I'm just feeling more and more worried about

01:25:14.130 --> 01:25:19.900
the books and about the politics of the knowing
them.

01:25:19.900 --> 01:25:25.130
You're worried about that?
Yes. It seems like we're embracing it. So

01:25:25.130 --> 01:25:31.710
a specific question to the architects-- and
we have here two architects who dealt with

01:25:31.710 --> 01:25:35.730
different emphasis and two different aesthetic
camps, perhaps-- hear immensely about the

01:25:35.730 --> 01:25:41.810
material and tectonic effects of their work.
So my question is, what happens when this

01:25:41.810 --> 01:25:46.100
aspect of your craft, for somebody to call
it a kind of care for detail, assembling your

01:25:46.100 --> 01:25:52.150
materials, the effects of those assemblies--
intersect with architecture of our program

01:25:52.150 --> 01:26:01.429
for learning. And here, I bring anything broadly--
library, museum, school. How do you sort of

01:26:01.429 --> 01:26:07.949
see the pressure of those programs and those
particular architectures on your own sense

01:26:07.949 --> 01:26:14.040
of practice and what have you?
[TALKING TOGETHER]

01:26:14.040 --> 01:26:16.730
[LAUGHTER]

01:26:16.730 --> 01:26:22.960
I don't see any contradiction at all, because
that's the kind of-- this is world that we

01:26:22.960 --> 01:26:29.389
negotiate. And it's actually the world that
we actually find stimulation from. Let's just

01:26:29.389 --> 01:26:34.580
talk about the stacks. Because that's the
elephant in the room.

01:26:34.580 --> 01:26:38.870
This is, for me, a question about, basically,
architecture itself produces knowledge. And

01:26:38.870 --> 01:26:44.130
I thought maybe that this is an unfair question,
because I think there's a right answer, right?

01:26:44.130 --> 01:26:56.090
I 
think there is a pressure on your architectures
in those situations to speak more like that.

01:26:56.090 --> 01:27:03.489
But the way that you asked the question suggests
as if we are these architects with visions,

01:27:03.489 --> 01:27:07.860
as if we don't do research just like you do
research.

01:27:07.860 --> 01:27:12.030
[INTERPOSING VOICES]
My question is, what's the--

01:27:12.030 --> 01:27:18.020
The part of the narrative, that I heard, is
that those people, like the Carol Terrys,

01:27:18.020 --> 01:27:24.820
like the 15 focus groups that we had in
Melbourne, completely changed the way that

01:27:24.820 --> 01:27:30.420
we would have organized space, situate relationships
between people, and not all of that is something

01:27:30.420 --> 01:27:37.210
you can articulate in a form like this, but
that the architecture is not naturally just

01:27:37.210 --> 01:27:44.150
what it is. It is a struggle to establish
that scenography, if I can put it that

01:27:44.150 --> 01:27:47.440
way.
The staging of these relationships is very

01:27:47.440 --> 01:27:54.239
much problematic. And then it finds other
ways in which it expresses itself through

01:27:54.239 --> 01:28:02.250
materials and hierarchies that effectively
register to your eye, or somebody else's eye,

01:28:02.250 --> 01:28:10.510
the icon or what seems to matter. But I think
of ourselves as incredibly nerdy when it comes

01:28:10.510 --> 01:28:18.080
to starting a project.
One thing you have to do is to unlearn-- to,

01:28:18.080 --> 01:28:24.969
in a way, eradicate your biases. Because everything
you thought you knew, you probably knew wrong.

01:28:24.969 --> 01:28:30.620
And every client group, or every culture,
or every geography, in a way, proves to you

01:28:30.620 --> 01:28:32.610
that you were wrong in what you thought you
knew.

01:28:32.610 --> 01:28:39.929
Does anyone want to comment on this? You're
like, OK, done with that one.

01:28:39.929 --> 01:28:47.150
No, I want to talk about stacks.
OK. So as a curator myself, I love the idea--

01:28:47.150 --> 01:28:53.880
and as someone who often might say designs,
knowledge as you would maybe say-- as part

01:28:53.880 --> 01:29:00.580
of my practice, right? I love the idea that
we could have a way of thinking in test kitchens,

01:29:00.580 --> 01:29:06.630
in libraries, or some form of experimental
library, wherever we might be going, that

01:29:06.630 --> 01:29:13.270
would include objects, et cetera, that would
have curated assemblies of knowledge objects,

01:29:13.270 --> 01:29:16.360
right?
And then we could rely on the librarian or

01:29:16.360 --> 01:29:23.230
some other kind of expert to produce those.
But as a researcher who relies on books--

01:29:23.230 --> 01:29:34.330
and all the other types of media, right? Maybe
you call it nostalgia, but I feel sort of

01:29:34.330 --> 01:29:39.639
stressed about the politics of access to books
without being patronized.

01:29:39.639 --> 01:29:46.389
There's something about arriving to a book
stack and not only feeling that kind of knowledge

01:29:46.389 --> 01:29:52.250
and being in awe about it, but actually being
able to find your way and curate it yourself

01:29:52.250 --> 01:30:00.570
as a researcher. So then, if we get rid of
storage-- because now it's just storage, and

01:30:00.570 --> 01:30:08.360
not a kind of political gesture-- what do
you do?

01:30:08.360 --> 01:30:16.320
So this is where I get into really magical
thinking. I mean, I love the experience of

01:30:16.320 --> 01:30:20.940
walking the stacks. And I believe serendipity
is pretty old. When people say, it doesn't

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:25.260
really happen, it's just nostalgia-- I've
seen it happen. It's real. It's not actually

01:30:25.260 --> 01:30:29.090
magic.
I think it's really powerful. But there are

01:30:29.090 --> 01:30:34.330
limits to the technology of a book, and the
technology of the book stack, right? When

01:30:34.330 --> 01:30:42.449
you navigate those stacks, MIT doesn't have
every book on your topic. So that act of physically

01:30:42.449 --> 01:30:48.560
browsing the stacks is incomplete.
And some books are checked out. And some of

01:30:48.560 --> 01:30:51.969
what you need isn't a book. It's a map. It's
data. It's images.

01:30:51.969 --> 01:30:59.100
And so in my wildest imagination, you can
wander the stacks in an augmented reality

01:30:59.100 --> 01:31:07.120
kind of way, so that you're having that visceral
experience of feeling and being in space with

01:31:07.120 --> 01:31:12.760
physical books, but you also have access in
some way that someone brilliant here at MIT

01:31:12.760 --> 01:31:19.260
will invent for us, to the digital objects
that are related to that. And to understanding

01:31:19.260 --> 01:31:25.280
what other people here at MIT are doing that's
related to that topic, so that you can then

01:31:25.280 --> 01:31:31.010
meet them in physical space somewhere. So,
my wildest imagination of the future-- and

01:31:31.010 --> 01:31:37.330
I think it's possible-- is that browsing the
stacks that we take what really works there--

01:31:37.330 --> 01:31:46.730
Yeah, I totally agree. A student in my library
test kitchen a couple of years ago developed

01:31:46.730 --> 01:31:51.030
a project around an idea that we had been
elaborating, which I continue to think is

01:31:51.030 --> 01:31:58.480
a really cool idea-- First of all, books today,
books that sit on our shelves, are not like

01:31:58.480 --> 01:32:03.570
the books that were produced fifty years ago.
They are already data objects before they

01:32:03.570 --> 01:32:09.690
are books. They are a radically different
kind of object in many ways than the books

01:32:09.690 --> 01:32:14.960
they look just like, except they're different.
But what if every time you searched for a

01:32:14.960 --> 01:32:21.020
term in a catalogue, the books-- whether it
was an object, or a set of objects-- they

01:32:21.020 --> 01:32:28.520
recognized you when you moved around in physical
space? That is a world of experiences that's

01:32:28.520 --> 01:32:34.239
not unlike the serendipitous experience,
but that's actually not a technological problem.

01:32:34.239 --> 01:32:39.469
It's a design problem. We could have our ID
tags and books that are plugged into networks

01:32:39.469 --> 01:32:48.969
that reflect the eccentric, peculiar intellectual
path thought by a single person. And I think

01:32:48.969 --> 01:32:52.340
thinking about how we design that experience--
You don't find that oppressive?

01:32:52.340 --> 01:32:58.750
[INTERPOSING VOICES]
It could be, but I think if any institution

01:32:58.750 --> 01:33:03.760
is gonna get it right, I think a library would
be that kind of institution that could really

01:33:03.760 --> 01:33:09.880
explore that world where the digital and the
analog intersect one another in ways that

01:33:09.880 --> 01:33:14.060
are productive. They are generative.
This is already happening when

01:33:14.060 --> 01:33:23.000
we get that unsolicited email by Pinterest
saying, these are the things that you're interested

01:33:23.000 --> 01:33:26.250
in.
I don't always welcome those, but I think

01:33:26.250 --> 01:33:31.000
that if they're properly filtered and so forth,
we can create those kinds of experiences and

01:33:31.000 --> 01:33:37.370
design them to be productive experiences.
So I'm excited about that idea. And I think

01:33:37.370 --> 01:33:46.030
that thinking about books-- even bookish objects
circulate in space. And if the traces of those

01:33:46.030 --> 01:33:50.909
invisible acts of looking to retrieve knowledge,
of putting knowledge forms together, were

01:33:50.909 --> 01:33:54.810
to become more visible, that's an enrichment.
I mean, I'm sure I'm not the only faculty

01:33:54.810 --> 01:34:01.050
member who loves to see what graduate students--
the information they're curating at their

01:34:01.050 --> 01:34:05.949
carrels when they're writing dissertations.
Because I discover things when I see them.

01:34:05.949 --> 01:34:09.850
Of course, a lot of that activity now is disappearing
into screens. And I can't see that anymore.

01:34:09.850 --> 01:34:16.280
And that's a loss for the collective understanding
of who we are, what we're doing, what people

01:34:16.280 --> 01:34:21.370
are thinking about. So, that kind of making
it visible, I think, is one of the kind of

01:34:21.370 --> 01:34:23.520
core challenges.
Making it visible, making lost connections

01:34:23.520 --> 01:34:30.230
visible and knowing who's reading what on
my topic, those are very important things

01:34:30.230 --> 01:34:35.350
that libraries can do, and connections that
we can do, but to do so in a way that respects

01:34:35.350 --> 01:34:42.020
the privacy of the reader, which is what makes--Ginnie, I know you'll back me up--it's one of those like, deeply

01:34:42.020 --> 01:34:49.969
held librarian values. You say those sorts of things
about snooping in a graduate student's desk.

01:34:49.969 --> 01:34:53.590
I'm, like, no, you can't do that.
[LAUGHTER]

01:34:53.590 --> 01:34:57.630
These are open carrels.
[LAUGHTER]

01:34:57.630 --> 01:35:01.520
Everybody does it.
Well, there, okay then.

01:35:01.520 --> 01:35:08.179
I'm very excited about the idea that the fourth
book in the series that I've just read one,

01:35:08.179 --> 01:35:10.170
two, and three of will just magically come
to me.

01:35:10.170 --> 01:35:17.300
You can say hi.
No, that's how I see your knowing the person

01:35:17.300 --> 01:35:22.540
operate.
Do you want me to call time?

01:35:22.540 --> 01:35:34.110
Everyone is happy talking to us. So I
mean, some of my questions got spent but

01:35:34.110 --> 01:35:40.880
I did have-- just to get in the conversation,
but I wanted to ask you to engage in a kind

01:35:40.880 --> 01:35:48.020
of a futurological experiment of this will
kill that, and give us a version-- obviously,

01:35:48.020 --> 01:35:54.969
when Victor Hugo wrote the famous words, this
was a death pronouncement to architecture

01:35:54.969 --> 01:36:00.820
against the book, basically saying that architecture's
power to articulate, represent, and assimilate

01:36:00.820 --> 01:36:09.429
ideas was going to be replaced by the books
which seemed more permanent and faster.

01:36:09.429 --> 01:36:14.400
And I'm wondering, if we were to produce a
statement like that for today, what would

01:36:14.400 --> 01:36:21.550
be this and that? Or, you don't have to use
the death metaphor. But I think we were already

01:36:21.550 --> 01:36:27.170
sort of going towards-- if we were to be birthing
something as opposed to euthanizing, what

01:36:27.170 --> 01:36:34.800
would it be?
[LAUGHTER]

01:36:34.800 --> 01:36:44.570
I mean, how long to believe that the promises
of technology would allow us to unleash the

01:36:44.570 --> 01:36:49.750
democratic promise of education, that technology
could do that. It hasn't been realized yet.

01:36:49.750 --> 01:36:55.030
But I want to believe that we can, right?
And some of that has to do with, now we really

01:36:55.030 --> 01:37:01.330
can't-- the books are not these sacred things.
Knowledge is not only inscribed in limited

01:37:01.330 --> 01:37:07.090
numbers of books that you have to belong to
that community to have access to. Now they

01:37:07.090 --> 01:37:14.580
can be in your pocket. Can technology really
work to make that true universal knowledge

01:37:14.580 --> 01:37:26.360
accessible to all?
I think it's extremely exciting, because in

01:37:26.360 --> 01:37:32.239
a way, I think that the limits of printed
matter, of printing, and of creating a world

01:37:32.239 --> 01:37:37.690
where we print for every community, this idea
of stacks of paper, so that they can engage,

01:37:37.690 --> 01:37:47.380
I think it reached its zenith. And I think
even before the internet. I just

01:37:47.380 --> 01:37:52.619
think it's an unsustainable kind of image.
It's a kind of verified, slightly elitist

01:37:52.619 --> 01:37:57.580
image. And in a way--
Technology potentially is similar to that.

01:37:57.580 --> 01:38:04.420
Yes, and I think that technology is potentially
also an elitist image. You have, an opportunity

01:38:04.420 --> 01:38:11.440
to try now to create the kind of forum that
technology can operate in, right? It doesn't

01:38:11.440 --> 01:38:19.760
have a sense of aestheticism. I think we're
in the forefront of the thing. The image which

01:38:19.760 --> 01:38:26.239
should make a kind of-- For me, the library
becomes a new public square. It really profoundly

01:38:26.239 --> 01:38:31.949
becomes less about an object, a palace of
rarefied information, and becomes a forum

01:38:31.949 --> 01:38:35.449
of debate.
It's the place where the engagement is happening,

01:38:35.449 --> 01:38:39.929
the commentary's happening. If you look at
what's happening in your school, I mean, this

01:38:39.929 --> 01:38:46.190
idea that you can publish, and comment, and
debate, and receive information from somebody

01:38:46.190 --> 01:38:56.050
who might have the rarefied document, is magical,
and, I think, more reasonable in terms of

01:38:56.050 --> 01:39:02.440
a kind of potential future. More sustainable.
So I kind of see that as moral rightsizing

01:39:02.440 --> 01:39:05.119
in the end.
In the future that I want to see, and what

01:39:05.119 --> 01:39:11.340
I want to get rid of, is I want the future
where just as when you go into a library,

01:39:11.340 --> 01:39:18.110
you go looking for something that's for you,
exactly right, and find it. And I want that

01:39:18.110 --> 01:39:25.670
applied to every child in this country and
to every person. Whatever the limits of their

01:39:25.670 --> 01:39:32.889
opportunities to learn, we in libraries find
a way to augment that. And that's the future

01:39:32.889 --> 01:39:39.170
I'd like to see us work to create.
I want to bring the senses back into this,

01:39:39.170 --> 01:39:47.710
because I did that slide with the words and
the image. But also the technology is doing

01:39:47.710 --> 01:39:53.600
very incredible things, so that what used
to be compartmentalized in terms of those

01:39:53.600 --> 01:39:59.590
who have hearing impairments, or those who
cannot see-- all of that is now coming back

01:39:59.590 --> 01:40:06.530
into a knowledge base that could be acoustic,
it could be visual, it could be smell. I'm

01:40:06.530 --> 01:40:11.280
thinking of, if you remember, last year or
the year before when Ferran Adriá came here.

01:40:11.280 --> 01:40:18.650
He was not talking about cooking. He was talking
about the entire knowledge, the alchemy, of

01:40:18.650 --> 01:40:24.290
what constitutes cooking.
And the idea that you would think of a special

01:40:24.290 --> 01:40:29.150
institute for that is in a way ridiculous
when he is already making the library that

01:40:29.150 --> 01:40:39.940
is everywhere. So how do you expand that database
to engage directly with your senses? That,

01:40:39.940 --> 01:40:45.360
to me, is fascinating.
I love all of those responses. I think what

01:40:45.360 --> 01:40:54.179
they suggest is, that some of these choices
are not mutually exclusive choices. The media

01:40:54.179 --> 01:40:59.670
historian in me trained as a medievalist immediately
kicks in these conversations in answer to

01:40:59.670 --> 01:41:07.510
Anna's provocation-- media never go away.
So the real questions always have to do with

01:41:07.510 --> 01:41:13.679
mixing and matching hierarchies.
We have such a proliferation of channels from

01:41:13.679 --> 01:41:18.780
a sensory standpoint, which we've touched
upon. So the question of what a library is

01:41:18.780 --> 01:41:24.340
and what it does is just undergoing this kind
of constant expansion over the course of its

01:41:24.340 --> 01:41:30.840
modern history, certainly. And the conventions
for how we organize the experience of accessing

01:41:30.840 --> 01:41:35.150
knowledge, I think those are all open for
design in a way that they haven't been for

01:41:35.150 --> 01:41:39.170
a couple of centuries.
This sort of legacy system, including the

01:41:39.170 --> 01:41:45.219
stacks, was the result of a very constructive
moment in history of ideas about access, about

01:41:45.219 --> 01:41:52.489
assembly, and so forth. And I think some of
the libraries that really excite me are libraries

01:41:52.489 --> 01:41:58.949
much like the one you just mentioned, of materials
materials, things that take us into a different

01:41:58.949 --> 01:42:04.420
kind of imaginary space, at least, of what
it is to experience or share or communicate.

01:42:04.420 --> 01:42:08.699
The image that you shared was more like a
cabinet of wonders.

01:42:08.699 --> 01:42:14.310
It was. 
Exactly. And maybe the Wunderkammer is, we

01:42:14.310 --> 01:42:20.139
don't want to give up the modes of access
and reliability that I think you were implying

01:42:20.139 --> 01:42:27.250
in your reference to the stack, because that
stands for a certain kind of rigor, certain

01:42:27.250 --> 01:42:33.210
forms of organization. But I actually think
that's where technology can really be a creative

01:42:33.210 --> 01:42:39.330
tool for having it both ways. You can have
programmable stacks where information is still

01:42:39.330 --> 01:42:45.199
accessible. Digital objects can exist in multiple
places at the same time.

01:42:45.199 --> 01:42:48.440
I was studying, I was programming it.
[LAUGHTER]

01:42:48.440 --> 01:42:53.199
You want to be the programmer.
For yourself, you are.

01:42:53.199 --> 01:43:00.159
Should we open it to the audience? There's
a lot of you. We could have questions from

01:43:00.159 --> 01:43:06.010
the audience. There are two mics on each side,
and if you could come to them or someone will

01:43:06.010 --> 01:43:18.239
bring it to you. But let us know if you have
any questions.

01:43:18.239 --> 01:43:25.170
Yes.
Leaving is also okay.

01:43:25.170 --> 01:43:34.429
[LAUGHTER]
Hey. Thank you for coming and speaking for

01:43:34.429 --> 01:43:43.790
us. My question is, that I'm interested in the library,
the act of the collection as an act of curation,

01:43:43.790 --> 01:43:49.940
and that curation as an act of gate-keeping.
So I'm interested how you program in a more

01:43:49.940 --> 01:43:56.889
open sense of curation. Because you can have
all the contemporary amenities in the world

01:43:56.889 --> 01:44:04.460
in a library and still come out with the same
forms of knowledge, modes of understanding.

01:44:04.460 --> 01:44:11.510
So how do you break that model of gate-keeping
and open it up to the community and program

01:44:11.510 --> 01:44:19.969
that into an integrative structure of a library?
You want to go first, or do you want me to--

01:44:19.969 --> 01:44:25.139
Why don't you go first?
I think we-- you're absolutely about that.

01:44:25.139 --> 01:44:30.060
In a way, we've touched on that a couple of
times here talking about that crazy idea of

01:44:30.060 --> 01:44:35.070
Jeffrey's that instead of following the book,
you're following the person and what their

01:44:35.070 --> 01:44:41.639
knowledge source is. In public libraries we
do it in a pretty real and pragmatic way.

01:44:41.639 --> 01:44:47.369
So for example, if you want a book at most
of the libraries where I have been, and we

01:44:47.369 --> 01:44:51.600
don't own it, we will get it.
We might borrow it from somebody. We more

01:44:51.600 --> 01:44:58.929
likely will buy it if it's available and can
be bought. We also follow, as much as we can,

01:44:58.929 --> 01:45:04.949
the patterns of individuals and groups within
our community and think, what are we getting

01:45:04.949 --> 01:45:07.760
for them?
And so for example, if I'm at a library that

01:45:07.760 --> 01:45:13.330
has a tremendous number of kids under the
age of five, what would happen to the picture

01:45:13.330 --> 01:45:18.280
book collection is very different than-- you
probably have a very small picture book collection

01:45:18.280 --> 01:45:20.369
here, right?
[LAUGHTER]

01:45:20.369 --> 01:45:26.239
So, we're not getting to the individual but

01:45:26.239 --> 01:45:33.060
we're getting to individuals in that way that,
I think, does it. The fact that it's the public's

01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:38.920
library means in many ways we're-- I hate
to use this buzzword-- we're crowd-sourcing

01:45:38.920 --> 01:45:43.330
what people are interested in, in having these
programs, and having this or that part of

01:45:43.330 --> 01:45:48.520
the collection, and having what's offered,
and really trying to be responsive in that

01:45:48.520 --> 01:45:53.320
way. We don't succeed every time, for sure,
but we're working on it.

01:45:53.320 --> 01:45:58.460
Yeah, I think another bit of what I think
you're getting at is, the act of duration,

01:45:58.460 --> 01:46:05.510
the act of selecting what's going to be in
what is a limited physical space, is a political

01:46:05.510 --> 01:46:12.469
act. It is, absolutely. People talk about
libraries as these great neutral spaces. They're

01:46:12.469 --> 01:46:23.350
not neutral. They've never been neutral.
Libraries, as an institution, are, by nature,

01:46:23.350 --> 01:46:30.010
very political. The idea that the public gets to have access. We're going to buy a couple of copies of this

01:46:30.010 --> 01:46:34.290
book, and anyone that wants it can come in
and have it. That's pretty downright socialist,

01:46:34.290 --> 01:46:35.830
really.
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:46:35.830 --> 01:46:41.270
Books sit next to each other on the shelves
with ideas over which people have shed blood.

01:46:41.270 --> 01:46:48.610
Right there, yes. And so libraries have certainly
played this gatekeeper role in many, many

01:46:48.610 --> 01:46:57.699
ways. Do we still do that to some extent?
Some of my colleagues in libraries say, you

01:46:57.699 --> 01:47:02.739
know, we're still trying to be gatekeepers
even though the fence is gone. And we can

01:47:02.739 --> 01:47:06.840
get to everything. So we're standing by this
gate while everybody's just going around us

01:47:06.840 --> 01:47:10.110
and getting everything.
But once they get to those things, some of

01:47:10.110 --> 01:47:14.719
those information objects are still locked
away. You still need a pass-code of some kind

01:47:14.719 --> 01:47:22.280
to get to them. And so for me, I don't want
to be a gatekeeper. I don't want to be a gatekeeper.

01:47:22.280 --> 01:47:24.610
I don't want libraries to be gatekeepers at
all.

01:47:24.610 --> 01:47:31.389
I want libraries to be these spaces that invite
everyone in to partake of the knowledge. One

01:47:31.389 --> 01:47:37.429
of the ways that some folks in research libraries
are talking about, flipping the collection,

01:47:37.429 --> 01:47:43.980
is-- what my research libraries have done
historically, on college campuses, is we've

01:47:43.980 --> 01:47:48.230
gone out and we've collected all the books
we think the students and the faculty at our

01:47:48.230 --> 01:47:53.300
campus want. We put them in buildings.
And we've let you come in and get stuff that

01:47:53.300 --> 01:47:57.560
other people have written, and research that
other people have done, and you can have access

01:47:57.560 --> 01:48:02.000
to it. And then you write your stuff and you
publish it somewhere, and those other libraries

01:48:02.000 --> 01:48:05.190
get copies of that, right?
And so what we're talking about now is sort

01:48:05.190 --> 01:48:10.830
of turning the libraries inside out. So the
libraries aren't just for bringing in research

01:48:10.830 --> 01:48:15.900
from outside of MIT so that people at MIT
teach and consume it, but it's actually using

01:48:15.900 --> 01:48:21.650
the libraries to push the research of MIT
out to the world, to flip it out, so we're

01:48:21.650 --> 01:48:24.679
not a gatekeeper anymore. I don't know what
the metaphor is.

01:48:24.679 --> 01:48:30.489
But we're throwing our research out there
to everyone. That's what I hope we will become--

01:48:30.489 --> 01:48:36.320
and that technology and buildings, right?
I mean, there's a way to signal that with

01:48:36.320 --> 01:48:41.000
architecture that I think could be really
cool.

01:48:41.000 --> 01:48:46.369
A point, and then a question that could be

01:48:46.369 --> 01:48:51.020
a comment on that the book
as an object is a pretty exquisite thing.

01:48:51.020 --> 01:48:55.719
So it'd be sad to lose that, or you don't
think the screen is a very good replacement.

01:48:55.719 --> 01:49:00.330
And the part about the stacks-- it's not necessarily
that the stack is a useful object, but the

01:49:00.330 --> 01:49:04.989
serendipity of the stack.
Remember, I'm doing research when I'm in the

01:49:04.989 --> 01:49:09.969
library and I'm looking at the shelf, I'll
often notice a few shelves down there's a

01:49:09.969 --> 01:49:13.159
different version of the topic I'm looking
at that's really important, that I didn't

01:49:13.159 --> 01:49:18.500
know about. And so that experience, I think,
is very important and would be sad to lose.

01:49:18.500 --> 01:49:23.340
But the more interesting question is this
notion of the library being the public square.

01:49:23.340 --> 01:49:28.929
It seems like there's a lot of pressure on
the library to be things that we used to have,

01:49:28.929 --> 01:49:34.150
like public squares.
[LAUGHTER]

01:49:34.150 --> 01:49:39.739
That opens a really powerful question. Because
actually, a lot of what we used to have is

01:49:39.739 --> 01:49:46.800
gone. And we don't have it anymore. All those
things that used to be those forums of engagement

01:49:46.800 --> 01:49:53.719
and those places where the connectivity happens
have become privatized or taken away.

01:49:53.719 --> 01:49:59.280
And that's the difficulty. That's the pressure
on the library. It's interesting that interior

01:49:59.280 --> 01:50:07.230
space is become a kind of metaphor for exterior
space. Because exterior is basically commodified

01:50:07.230 --> 01:50:16.199
and sold. That's just real.
I mean, is there any way in architecture to

01:50:16.199 --> 01:50:20.540
somehow gesture that there's that lack? I
mean like, providing something that there

01:50:20.540 --> 01:50:26.820
should already be.
Well I think that there's politics, which

01:50:26.820 --> 01:50:32.139
is a different thing. We don't do that. But
I think in architecture we can respond to

01:50:32.139 --> 01:50:36.590
this.
We're interested in the city. I mean, we're

01:50:36.590 --> 01:50:40.570
talking about libraries and everything, but
I'm interested fundamentally in the human

01:50:40.570 --> 01:50:45.909
being in the city with this mechanism that
we invented 10,000 years ago, and how it supports

01:50:45.909 --> 01:50:51.040
our life. And that we had this extraordinary
densification that's changed everything, because

01:50:51.040 --> 01:50:56.429
we have mass migration to the city. And so
the nature of how we access information, how

01:50:56.429 --> 01:51:01.690
we play the social aspect, has completely
changed. And we're not gonna go backwards

01:51:01.690 --> 01:51:04.099
unless we destroy the city and invent something
else.

01:51:04.099 --> 01:51:10.320
That's a different project. Now we have to
kind of create these spaces that allow these

01:51:10.320 --> 01:51:15.080
opportunities to happen. And the library just
kind of found itself moving from just kind

01:51:15.080 --> 01:51:20.980
of being a palace of knowledge and of the
elite, the 1%, to becoming the place where

01:51:20.980 --> 01:51:28.409
actually the greatest amount of our community
believe in education as a way to create citizens--

01:51:28.409 --> 01:51:34.050
has become the lens by which we kind of throw
people through it into the world. So it's

01:51:34.050 --> 01:51:38.670
transformed. It's just transforms into the
nature of the way the city is, and the way

01:51:38.670 --> 01:51:44.810
in which densification is.
I love the library as a kind of romantic book

01:51:44.810 --> 01:51:49.580
stack, but that's just to do with the last
200 years, or 100 years. I mean, it's getting

01:51:49.580 --> 01:51:57.840
bigger than that. It's really a lens for creating
people, creating an elite, like in monasteries,

01:51:57.840 --> 01:52:02.400
et cetera. It's now a lens for creating the
greatest amount of educated people that we

01:52:02.400 --> 01:52:10.710
can make.
So if that's its DNA, let's not get caught up on--For me, I'm just

01:52:10.710 --> 01:52:17.420
like, let's get the-- and what I'm interested--
I mean, OK, we can get het up on things, that's

01:52:17.420 --> 01:52:24.989
fine. But I'm more interested in the urgency
of integrating models that create the greatest

01:52:24.989 --> 01:52:33.750
amount of having the most fun.
I'm an archivist but also an avid sci-fi reader.

01:52:33.750 --> 01:52:40.219
And as you were talking about the research
we did, I was just thinking about these things,

01:52:40.219 --> 01:52:45.219
necessarily. And it really struck me, thinking
about the lessons in sci-fi. Going back to

01:52:45.219 --> 01:52:49.110
a hundred years ago, Einstein was saying these
things don't exist. And now, gosh, did anybody

01:52:49.110 --> 01:52:51.940
know that they really are.
And so just thinking about the lessons of

01:52:51.940 --> 01:52:57.730
history, but also the lessons of design of
libraries, design of those spaces in science

01:52:57.730 --> 01:53:04.179
fiction over time. And using that, are there
architects, people who design, that are particularly

01:53:04.179 --> 01:53:09.670
looking to add-- how do we design something
in a sci-fi book that is placed 300 years

01:53:09.670 --> 01:53:14.520
in the future, on a different planet? And
does that come into the conversation on thinking

01:53:14.520 --> 01:53:21.170
about current design?
Who's the client for that?

01:53:21.170 --> 01:53:24.949
It was an architecture question.
What do you guys think about sci-fi in architecture?

01:53:24.949 --> 01:53:33.440
Some people are really engaged in sci-fi architecture.
We're not the ones, but--

01:53:33.440 --> 01:53:38.239
We're at MIT, though, the home of sensible cities, that was sci-fi

01:53:38.239 --> 01:53:45.320
just a few years ago. So yes, it's very much
alive and well. The question is where it goes

01:53:45.320 --> 01:53:48.400
next.
Well I don't know if the movie, Mission Impossible,

01:53:48.400 --> 01:53:53.150
counts as sci-fi, but that scene where Tom
Cruise is spinning the information around--

01:53:53.150 --> 01:53:57.520
I know that the actual--

01:53:57.520 --> 01:54:03.630
That's my vision.
All right, I got a signal that we have to

01:54:03.630 --> 01:54:15.000
finish. So thank you very much, our panelists,
and thank you everyone.

01:54:15.000 --> 01:54:21.660
[APPLAUSE]

